1
00:00:40,459 --> 00:00:41,627
A face...

2
00:00:44,630 --> 00:00:45,923
...a fleet...

3
00:00:48,509 --> 00:00:49,593
...a war...

4
00:00:53,305 --> 00:00:54,473
...a man...

5
00:00:57,684 --> 00:00:59,061
...a thought...

6
00:01:02,231 --> 00:01:03,190
...a trick...

7
00:01:06,276 --> 00:01:08,612
...a trick to break the walls of Troy.

8
00:01:11,156 --> 00:01:13,909
And burn it screaming to the ground!

9
00:02:05,419 --> 00:02:06,587
What does it mean?

10
00:02:15,345 --> 00:02:16,597
A gift.

11
00:02:19,766 --> 00:02:21,101
For Athena.

12
00:02:24,146 --> 00:02:25,397
Common gods.

13
00:02:26,273 --> 00:02:27,608
Respect their offering.

14
00:02:31,862 --> 00:02:32,821
Save it from the waves!

15
00:02:33,405 --> 00:02:35,073
Take it to Athena's temple.

16
00:02:35,908 --> 00:02:37,367
The war is over.

17
00:02:44,666 --> 00:02:45,626
A face...

18
00:02:49,296 --> 00:02:50,881
...a fleet...

19
00:02:52,925 --> 00:02:54,176
...a war...

20
00:02:57,262 --> 00:02:58,764
...a man...

21
00:02:58,847 --> 00:02:59,848
Stop!

22
00:03:04,436 --> 00:03:05,646
Not this song.

23
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:12,694
Mommy's boy!

24
00:03:12,778 --> 00:03:14,988
Run, run, little man!

25
00:03:15,530 --> 00:03:16,531
Run!

26
00:03:17,574 --> 00:03:19,952
I wanna remind them of whose
wine they're drinking.

27
00:03:20,035 --> 00:03:21,828
I don't want Odysseus' song.

28
00:03:21,912 --> 00:03:22,996
I want Odysseus.

29
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:24,539
Then why do you entertain suitors

30
00:03:24,623 --> 00:03:26,083
in his house night after night?

31
00:03:26,833 --> 00:03:31,004
In this world, a man does what he chooses.

32
00:03:31,088 --> 00:03:32,172
I do what I can.

33
00:03:32,256 --> 00:03:34,967
I am a man now, Mom.

34
00:03:35,050 --> 00:03:36,969
So throw them out.

35
00:03:37,803 --> 00:03:39,554
Break Zeus' Law
and see what they do to you

36
00:03:39,638 --> 00:03:41,098
if you give them that excuse.

37
00:03:46,895 --> 00:03:49,314
I've already lost a husband.

38
00:03:49,398 --> 00:03:51,108
I don't wanna lose a son.

39
00:03:51,191 --> 00:03:52,359
But Dad's coming back.

40
00:03:52,442 --> 00:03:53,944
Then where is he?

41
00:03:54,027 --> 00:03:56,989
Eight years since Troy fell.

42
00:03:57,072 --> 00:03:58,907
Almost 20 since he left us.

43
00:03:58,991 --> 00:04:00,033
Where is he?

44
00:04:11,712 --> 00:04:14,089
We have to welcome him
like we welcomed all of you.

45
00:04:14,172 --> 00:04:15,465
Are you calling us beggars?

46
00:04:15,549 --> 00:04:18,093
I'm saying you've been
welcome here for three years.

47
00:04:18,176 --> 00:04:21,263
We've only enjoyed
your hospitality for so long,

48
00:04:22,973 --> 00:04:24,599
because your mother won't choose.

49
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:26,310
She's already married.

50
00:04:26,393 --> 00:04:27,853
Odysseus is dead.

51
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:30,272
What do you know about my dad?

52
00:04:32,733 --> 00:04:34,109
I know he kept stringy cattle

53
00:04:34,192 --> 00:04:35,694
and bad wine.

54
00:04:35,777 --> 00:04:38,322
You had the best of it
the first two seasons.

55
00:04:38,405 --> 00:04:39,990
And I know he never came home.

56
00:04:40,073 --> 00:04:41,366
Not yet.

57
00:04:42,951 --> 00:04:46,580
You're pining for a daddy
you didn't even know.

58
00:04:46,663 --> 00:04:48,665
Like some sniveling bastard.

59
00:04:48,749 --> 00:04:51,752
Well, since none of us can really know
who our fathers are,

60
00:04:51,835 --> 00:04:53,420
don't claim to know mine.

61
00:04:53,503 --> 00:04:54,629
But I did know him.

62
00:04:54,713 --> 00:04:57,466
As boys. We watched him hunt.

63
00:04:59,551 --> 00:05:01,678
I tried to volunteer
for his expedition to Troy,

64
00:05:01,762 --> 00:05:03,263
but he told me to stay here

65
00:05:03,347 --> 00:05:04,639
and keep an eye on you.

66
00:05:04,723 --> 00:05:06,600
On me, or my mother?

67
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:09,686
You can't blame me for looking.

68
00:05:09,770 --> 00:05:11,063
My dad is coming home.

69
00:05:11,146 --> 00:05:13,982
And his hospitality
won't be as generous as hers.

70
00:05:14,066 --> 00:05:16,068
So he doesn't follow Zeus' Law, then?

71
00:05:16,151 --> 00:05:19,071
Zeus' Law tells us
to treat strangers as we'd be treated

72
00:05:19,154 --> 00:05:21,323
because they might be gods in disguise.

73
00:05:21,948 --> 00:05:24,576
I think we've established, Antinous,
that you are not a god.

74
00:05:25,869 --> 00:05:27,829
This one certainly
doesn't smell like a god.

75
00:05:27,913 --> 00:05:29,539
Wouldn't be much of a disguise if he did.

76
00:05:29,623 --> 00:05:30,999
Careful.

77
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:33,752
He might strike you down. Right now.

78
00:05:39,674 --> 00:05:41,009
Come on.

79
00:05:47,516 --> 00:05:49,351
That thunder was luck.

80
00:05:50,102 --> 00:05:52,979
I don't know. You have wise eyes.

81
00:05:54,022 --> 00:05:55,524
Athena's eyes. Here.

82
00:05:55,607 --> 00:05:57,484
You sailed with traders?

83
00:05:57,567 --> 00:05:58,985
From Sparta.

84
00:05:59,069 --> 00:06:00,946
Has Menelaus come home from the war?

85
00:06:02,030 --> 00:06:05,033
Years ago. Odysseus never came back?

86
00:06:05,117 --> 00:06:06,326
We don't even know if he's alive.

87
00:06:06,410 --> 00:06:07,452
Have you heard anything?

88
00:06:07,536 --> 00:06:09,329
The song of how he won the war.

89
00:06:09,413 --> 00:06:10,997
We've all heard that. Anything else?

90
00:06:11,498 --> 00:06:12,541
No.

91
00:06:13,250 --> 00:06:17,838
But Sparta is full of Trojan War veterans.

92
00:06:17,921 --> 00:06:19,840
Someone'll know something.

93
00:06:19,923 --> 00:06:23,510
You're Odysseus' son? Go to Sparta.

94
00:06:23,593 --> 00:06:26,096
Go. See Menelaus.

95
00:06:26,763 --> 00:06:27,764
He'd welcome you.

96
00:06:29,891 --> 00:06:31,977
The Queen wants to speak to the stranger.

97
00:06:33,478 --> 00:06:35,105
She lives for news.

98
00:06:38,024 --> 00:06:39,526
Just don't try to give her any false hope.

99
00:06:39,609 --> 00:06:40,861
She has some way of knowing

100
00:06:40,944 --> 00:06:43,321
whether a person
has actually seen Odysseus.

101
00:06:43,405 --> 00:06:44,573
You see that man?

102
00:06:44,656 --> 00:06:46,741
He can't see much anymore,
but that doesn't stop him

103
00:06:46,825 --> 00:06:48,493
beating travelers to death

104
00:06:48,577 --> 00:06:50,704
in exchange for lies about my father.

105
00:07:17,522 --> 00:07:19,858
No, no, too fast.

106
00:07:20,484 --> 00:07:21,568
How can you know?

107
00:07:21,651 --> 00:07:25,155
You don't need eyes
to hear a hasty combination.

108
00:07:25,238 --> 00:07:26,698
Mentor, push him.

109
00:07:34,873 --> 00:07:36,082
No, no. Too fast.

110
00:07:45,759 --> 00:07:47,093
He's as friendly as you.

111
00:07:47,177 --> 00:07:49,930
He only
answered to your father.

112
00:07:50,013 --> 00:07:51,598
Where'd he get him from?

113
00:07:51,681 --> 00:07:53,600
They met right here.

114
00:07:58,605 --> 00:08:01,024
Come on.

115
00:08:01,107 --> 00:08:02,108
Aw.

116
00:08:02,776 --> 00:08:04,027
I'll take him.

117
00:08:04,110 --> 00:08:05,111
For your boy?

118
00:08:05,195 --> 00:08:06,530
For a hunting dog.

119
00:08:08,365 --> 00:08:09,616
Damned if he wasn't right.

120
00:08:45,193 --> 00:08:47,988
Hold still. How bad a scar do you want?

121
00:08:49,656 --> 00:08:51,491
Why do you always pluck your bow?

122
00:08:51,575 --> 00:08:52,576
It's only fair.

123
00:08:52,659 --> 00:08:54,452
To warn your prey?

124
00:08:54,536 --> 00:08:55,620
To fight with honor.

125
00:08:55,704 --> 00:08:57,581
But you weren't fighting.
You were hunting.

126
00:08:57,664 --> 00:08:59,583
Ah, hunting's nothing
but fighting with stealth.

127
00:09:00,083 --> 00:09:01,334
Clever.

128
00:09:01,418 --> 00:09:04,087
Clever, but your cleverness
gonna get you in trouble.

129
00:09:04,170 --> 00:09:05,130
Try again.

130
00:09:05,839 --> 00:09:08,091
This time, not as fast or precise.

131
00:09:08,174 --> 00:09:10,719
You can't win with a perfect defense.

132
00:09:10,802 --> 00:09:11,928
Draw the attack.

133
00:09:12,012 --> 00:09:13,888
- How?
- Hesitate.

134
00:09:13,972 --> 00:09:16,558
Open your belly to your opponent's blade.

135
00:09:16,641 --> 00:09:18,310
Their attack becomes your chance.

136
00:09:50,216 --> 00:09:51,676
You teach my father that?

137
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:54,346
No, no, he taught me.

138
00:09:54,429 --> 00:09:56,181
He said, "You're not always

139
00:09:56,264 --> 00:09:58,558
going to be the strongest or the fastest,

140
00:09:58,642 --> 00:10:01,686
so be... Be the smartest."

141
00:10:01,770 --> 00:10:03,188
What would he do, huh?

142
00:10:03,271 --> 00:10:05,690
If he came back here
to find all these suitors in his house?

143
00:10:05,774 --> 00:10:09,194
Don't. I can't even let myself
imagine what he'd do to them.

144
00:10:09,277 --> 00:10:10,820
There are so many.

145
00:10:10,904 --> 00:10:13,073
Cut a couple down, the others cave.

146
00:10:13,156 --> 00:10:15,116
Bullies and cowards.

147
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:16,826
Most of them were old enough
to go to Troy,

148
00:10:16,910 --> 00:10:18,536
but when they saw Agamemnon's sail...

149
00:10:22,374 --> 00:10:23,750
...their fathers hid them.

150
00:10:27,379 --> 00:10:28,880
And he knew Agamemnon's war

151
00:10:28,963 --> 00:10:31,299
would take many years and many lives.

152
00:10:36,304 --> 00:10:38,598
So he raised his army by lottery.

153
00:10:41,476 --> 00:10:42,519
- Name?
- Sinon.

154
00:10:42,602 --> 00:10:44,396
Draw. No.

155
00:10:44,479 --> 00:10:45,730
- Name?
- Antinous.

156
00:10:45,814 --> 00:10:46,856
Draw.

157
00:10:48,441 --> 00:10:49,359
Yes.

158
00:10:49,442 --> 00:10:50,610
By lots?

159
00:10:53,279 --> 00:10:55,448
Antinous told me he volunteered.

160
00:10:55,532 --> 00:10:57,117
I think
they're up to something.

161
00:11:05,583 --> 00:11:07,544
Your boy's trying to take Sinon's place.

162
00:11:08,545 --> 00:11:09,963
I'm not gonna let him.

163
00:11:10,046 --> 00:11:11,881
He needs to stay here.
Take care of his family.

164
00:11:14,926 --> 00:11:16,302
He can watch over your boy too.

165
00:11:16,386 --> 00:11:17,637
Of course.

166
00:11:21,558 --> 00:11:22,642
He's with me.

167
00:11:23,143 --> 00:11:25,353
He wanted Antinous to look after me?

168
00:11:25,437 --> 00:11:26,896
No, no, no.

169
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:29,691
But Odysseus was sensitive
to a father's shame.

170
00:11:29,774 --> 00:11:31,443
He felt for these fathers.

171
00:11:31,526 --> 00:11:34,612
You and your mother meant more
to him than promises of glory.

172
00:11:36,156 --> 00:11:37,323
So he knew it would be years.

173
00:11:38,950 --> 00:11:40,660
Did he tell my mother?

174
00:11:40,744 --> 00:11:42,162
That's a question for her.

175
00:11:42,245 --> 00:11:45,457
Eumaeus, is he ever coming back?

176
00:11:46,833 --> 00:11:48,126
Argus thinks so.

177
00:11:48,209 --> 00:11:50,378
Never known a dog reach 20.

178
00:11:51,713 --> 00:11:53,006
But he's long outlived his master.

179
00:12:10,315 --> 00:12:11,733
I didn't mean to stop you.

180
00:12:14,944 --> 00:12:16,404
I'm in no hurry.

181
00:12:17,489 --> 00:12:20,784
The elders decreed when I finish
your grandfather's burial shroud,

182
00:12:20,867 --> 00:12:22,243
it'll be time to remarry.

183
00:12:22,327 --> 00:12:23,369
Why?

184
00:12:29,083 --> 00:12:31,044
Ithaca's been
without a king too long.

185
00:12:31,878 --> 00:12:33,296
They want security.

186
00:12:33,379 --> 00:12:34,380
From what?

187
00:12:34,464 --> 00:12:36,424
The people from the sea.

188
00:12:36,508 --> 00:12:38,760
That's just traveler's tales.

189
00:12:38,843 --> 00:12:40,678
All we know of the world
is traveler's tales.

190
00:12:40,762 --> 00:12:42,555
That's how we found out Troy had fallen.

191
00:12:43,139 --> 00:12:44,390
Now they all tell stories

192
00:12:44,474 --> 00:12:46,518
of attacks by people from the sea.

193
00:12:47,602 --> 00:12:49,479
And who are the people from the sea?

194
00:12:49,562 --> 00:12:52,690
No one knows, but the elders
want Ithaca to be prepared.

195
00:12:52,774 --> 00:12:53,817
Prepared?

196
00:12:53,900 --> 00:12:56,653
We're part of the greatest
civilization ever known.

197
00:12:58,488 --> 00:13:00,114
Look at this great house.

198
00:13:00,198 --> 00:13:01,950
Stones as old as knowledge.

199
00:13:02,033 --> 00:13:05,745
At the mercy of some greedy,
unwanted guests?

200
00:13:05,829 --> 00:13:07,831
The structure's nothing

201
00:13:08,748 --> 00:13:10,375
without people's respect for its meaning.

202
00:13:11,125 --> 00:13:12,126
Zeus' Law.

203
00:13:13,127 --> 00:13:15,129
Treat others as you would be treated.

204
00:13:15,213 --> 00:13:18,216
They abuse that, they destroy everything.

205
00:13:18,299 --> 00:13:20,969
So how could you
think of marrying one of them?

206
00:13:21,052 --> 00:13:23,471
Because, a long time ago,

207
00:13:24,806 --> 00:13:26,933
your father asked me to.

208
00:13:27,016 --> 00:13:28,518
Agamemnon is our king.

209
00:13:28,601 --> 00:13:31,145
And the Trojans took his brother's wife.

210
00:13:31,229 --> 00:13:33,273
Or she ran away with them.

211
00:13:33,356 --> 00:13:36,901
Either way. The walls of Troy
are said to be impregnable.

212
00:13:37,986 --> 00:13:40,488
And we'll sit outside that great city,

213
00:13:40,572 --> 00:13:42,365
trying to draw them into battle.

214
00:13:44,409 --> 00:13:47,370
Then years go by. And Telemachus grows.

215
00:13:47,996 --> 00:13:50,123
And you learn life without me.

216
00:13:50,206 --> 00:13:52,917
Menelaus will give up on Helen.

217
00:13:57,338 --> 00:13:59,215
This is Agamemnon's excuse

218
00:13:59,299 --> 00:14:02,552
to break Troy's control
of the trading routes.

219
00:14:04,512 --> 00:14:07,807
He won't let it pass. Ever.

220
00:14:14,522 --> 00:14:16,774
Well, what if you refuse?

221
00:14:22,530 --> 00:14:24,198
He'll take our son.

222
00:14:25,742 --> 00:14:27,160
He would never.

223
00:14:27,243 --> 00:14:29,370
He sacrificed his own daughter.

224
00:14:29,454 --> 00:14:31,247
For favorable winds.

225
00:14:32,540 --> 00:14:34,918
Why would the gods
want his daughter's life?

226
00:14:35,543 --> 00:14:37,420
The power of a sacrifice

227
00:14:38,880 --> 00:14:41,507
is in the cost to the person making it.

228
00:14:44,844 --> 00:14:46,679
That's monstrous.

229
00:14:49,098 --> 00:14:50,683
And committed.

230
00:14:53,519 --> 00:14:55,355
I won't return soon.

231
00:14:56,314 --> 00:14:57,649
If at all.

232
00:15:01,986 --> 00:15:03,655
What if we ran?

233
00:15:05,114 --> 00:15:07,116
Took your fastest ship.

234
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:08,409
And brightest crew.

235
00:15:08,493 --> 00:15:10,662
And headed for the horizon.

236
00:15:12,246 --> 00:15:16,834
Held hands on deck
as we rowed west at dusk,

237
00:15:16,918 --> 00:15:18,753
chasing the escaping sun.

238
00:15:22,382 --> 00:15:24,175
There's a world beyond these walls.

239
00:15:25,093 --> 00:15:30,515
Agamemnon's vengeance
will fall on all of Ithaca.

240
00:15:36,646 --> 00:15:37,897
Now promise me.

241
00:15:40,191 --> 00:15:42,276
If I don't return

242
00:15:42,360 --> 00:15:44,362
by the time Telemachus comes of age,

243
00:15:45,113 --> 00:15:46,447
promise me

244
00:15:47,699 --> 00:15:50,159
you'll take another husband.

245
00:15:51,703 --> 00:15:53,246
Enough sacrifices.

246
00:15:53,329 --> 00:15:55,081
Love's not a sacrifice.

247
00:15:55,164 --> 00:15:56,374
Of course it is.

248
00:15:57,667 --> 00:15:59,043
I don't want you to make it.

249
00:15:59,127 --> 00:16:01,963
It's not up to you.

250
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:04,132
Promise me.

251
00:16:05,717 --> 00:16:06,718
No.

252
00:16:10,555 --> 00:16:12,348
Promise me you'll come back.

253
00:16:17,895 --> 00:16:19,188
What if I can't?

254
00:16:25,153 --> 00:16:27,947
Then your memory
won't be making my decisions.

255
00:16:32,827 --> 00:16:34,787
Take my pin.

256
00:16:34,871 --> 00:16:36,039
Wear it always.

257
00:16:36,122 --> 00:16:38,624
Athena will bring you home.

258
00:16:38,708 --> 00:16:40,168
I'm not sure it suits me.

259
00:16:42,754 --> 00:16:45,339
When travelers claim to have seen you,

260
00:16:45,423 --> 00:16:47,425
I'll ask what you were wearing.

261
00:16:47,508 --> 00:16:49,135
And I'll know if they were lying.

262
00:16:52,472 --> 00:16:54,557
If the people from the sea are real,

263
00:16:55,767 --> 00:16:59,395
right now, Ithaca has no king
to raise an army.

264
00:16:59,979 --> 00:17:01,481
I could take the throne.

265
00:17:03,691 --> 00:17:04,859
Come.

266
00:17:16,662 --> 00:17:17,872
String it.

267
00:17:28,174 --> 00:17:29,884
That's not fair.

268
00:17:29,967 --> 00:17:31,969
You said only he could string this bow.

269
00:17:32,053 --> 00:17:33,846
He was happy to let others try.

270
00:17:36,265 --> 00:17:37,934
He'd line up all the axes.

271
00:17:39,477 --> 00:17:40,686
Yeah.

272
00:18:10,466 --> 00:18:11,968
No one else ever did it.

273
00:18:13,344 --> 00:18:16,389
Mom, they're bleeding us dry
so you'll be forced to choose one of them

274
00:18:16,472 --> 00:18:19,100
before I can claim the throne myself.

275
00:18:19,183 --> 00:18:21,561
The traveler told me
Menelaus returned years ago.

276
00:18:21,644 --> 00:18:23,062
They've all returned.

277
00:18:23,146 --> 00:18:24,730
All who survived.

278
00:18:26,232 --> 00:18:27,692
Maybe he is dead.

279
00:18:28,192 --> 00:18:30,278
And if he is, then this is my palace.

280
00:18:31,404 --> 00:18:32,405
We need to know.

281
00:18:32,488 --> 00:18:35,241
I have to go to Menelaus
to find out the truth.

282
00:18:35,324 --> 00:18:36,617
Telemachus,

283
00:18:37,451 --> 00:18:38,870
these suitors mean you harm.

284
00:18:38,953 --> 00:18:41,330
You think I don't know that?

285
00:18:41,414 --> 00:18:43,958
Every night, they push me
to give them the excuse they need.

286
00:18:44,041 --> 00:18:47,211
And out there, on the sea, on the road?

287
00:18:47,879 --> 00:18:50,047
They won't need an excuse.
You just won't come back.

288
00:18:52,383 --> 00:18:54,510
You don't want me
to claim the throne myself.

289
00:18:56,512 --> 00:19:00,349
You just wanna keep me in my place
so that you can stay Queen of Ithaca.

290
00:19:00,433 --> 00:19:03,144
I've had enough of being Queen of Ithaca
without my king!

291
00:19:03,227 --> 00:19:05,104
Without your king?

292
00:19:06,189 --> 00:19:07,190
Or "a" king?

293
00:19:08,858 --> 00:19:11,861
And you think you're ready
to take your father's place?

294
00:19:13,696 --> 00:19:14,906
You're a child.

295
00:19:25,374 --> 00:19:27,251
Too loud, Melanthius.

296
00:19:27,335 --> 00:19:29,086
Don't wear your spurs in the palace.

297
00:19:29,170 --> 00:19:31,172
You're a cowherd, not a horseman.

298
00:19:31,255 --> 00:19:32,673
And you're a pig farmer.

299
00:19:32,757 --> 00:19:35,134
And who are you
to talk to me like that? Hmm?

300
00:19:35,218 --> 00:19:38,596
The servant our master left
in charge of his herdsmen.

301
00:19:39,096 --> 00:19:42,725
Our master is long gone,
you blind old fool.

302
00:19:42,808 --> 00:19:45,269
And you're about as welcome
here as that dog is.

303
00:19:46,437 --> 00:19:47,480
Argus!

304
00:19:51,150 --> 00:19:52,193
Argus!

305
00:19:53,527 --> 00:19:54,904
Argus.

306
00:19:54,987 --> 00:19:56,197
Argus.

307
00:19:57,615 --> 00:19:59,200
Sir, he didn't do anything.

308
00:19:59,283 --> 00:20:00,701
So that stink is you?

309
00:20:01,953 --> 00:20:03,287
Leave the old dog alone.

310
00:20:03,371 --> 00:20:05,248
- Which one?
- Both of us.

311
00:20:06,499 --> 00:20:07,541
What did you say to me, slave?

312
00:20:07,625 --> 00:20:08,626
He just got carried away.

313
00:20:08,709 --> 00:20:10,544
Argus is my father's hunting dog.

314
00:20:10,628 --> 00:20:11,921
His hunting dog?

315
00:20:16,050 --> 00:20:17,343
Look at that thing.

316
00:20:18,386 --> 00:20:20,012
Get it out of here. We're trying to eat.

317
00:20:20,096 --> 00:20:22,098
Don't! Can't you see he's in pain?

318
00:20:22,181 --> 00:20:24,517
Okay. Then I'll put it out of its misery.

319
00:20:25,017 --> 00:20:26,560
Leave him alone! Argh!

320
00:20:43,869 --> 00:20:44,870
Did you see?

321
00:20:45,871 --> 00:20:47,206
You all see?

322
00:20:48,708 --> 00:20:49,959
He attacked me!

323
00:20:50,543 --> 00:20:51,544
I have the right!

324
00:20:52,295 --> 00:20:53,921
Please!

325
00:20:55,840 --> 00:20:57,425
Please don't hurt my son.

326
00:21:00,636 --> 00:21:02,513
Easy. Easy. Polybus.

327
00:21:03,097 --> 00:21:05,224
The young pup lost his head.
Show some mercy.

328
00:21:05,975 --> 00:21:08,894
Here. Drink some more of his wine.

329
00:21:08,978 --> 00:21:10,354
I'll take care of it.

330
00:21:10,938 --> 00:21:12,857
It's my father's hunting dog.

331
00:21:13,899 --> 00:21:15,860
He's only living to see Odysseus return.

332
00:21:15,943 --> 00:21:16,944
It's fine.

333
00:21:17,028 --> 00:21:18,654
I'll put him on the dung heap out front

334
00:21:18,738 --> 00:21:20,948
so he can greet his master
a little sooner.

335
00:21:31,083 --> 00:21:32,960
That dog stays out there.

336
00:21:34,712 --> 00:21:36,297
Next time, think twice
before you offer up your life

337
00:21:36,380 --> 00:21:37,715
for an animal.

338
00:21:49,185 --> 00:21:50,186
Eurycleia.

339
00:21:52,104 --> 00:21:53,898
Prepare supplies.
Take them down to the ships.

340
00:21:53,981 --> 00:21:55,024
Tell no one.

341
00:21:55,107 --> 00:21:56,442
Use only people you trust.

342
00:21:56,525 --> 00:21:58,027
You'll break your mother's heart.

343
00:21:58,110 --> 00:22:00,071
If I stay, they'll force me to fight.

344
00:22:00,154 --> 00:22:01,739
No one will back me.

345
00:22:01,822 --> 00:22:03,616
I have to find out
what happened to my father.

346
00:22:03,699 --> 00:22:07,328
Ithaca will be behind me
if they know Odysseus is dead.

347
00:22:07,411 --> 00:22:08,913
The suitors will never allow that.

348
00:22:08,996 --> 00:22:10,081
You will never make it back alive.

349
00:22:10,164 --> 00:22:14,043
Tell Mentor to meet me
at the ships just before dawn.

350
00:22:14,126 --> 00:22:16,837
What do you want me to tell your mother?

351
00:22:17,838 --> 00:22:19,590
To hold out for as long as she can.

352
00:22:23,260 --> 00:22:25,221
Ship's ready. Tide's right.

353
00:22:25,971 --> 00:22:28,391
Have I ever told
you that you have wise eyes?

354
00:22:28,474 --> 00:22:29,850
Athena's eyes?

355
00:22:29,934 --> 00:22:30,935
Once or twice.

356
00:22:31,018 --> 00:22:32,978
You haven't even asked me
where we're going yet.

357
00:22:33,062 --> 00:22:34,855
Where are we going?

358
00:22:34,939 --> 00:22:35,940
To find my dad.

359
00:22:36,649 --> 00:22:39,026
He's alive. I can feel it
in the wind and the waves.

360
00:22:39,110 --> 00:22:40,319
He's out there somewhere,

361
00:22:40,403 --> 00:22:42,571
wandering, injured, lost, I don't know.

362
00:22:44,031 --> 00:22:45,658
But he's alive.

363
00:23:16,897 --> 00:23:18,649
More dreams?

364
00:23:19,442 --> 00:23:21,068
Men drowning.

365
00:23:21,152 --> 00:23:22,194
Inside.

366
00:23:23,487 --> 00:23:27,283
Some dark space.

367
00:23:29,618 --> 00:23:32,163
Firelight on stone. I don't know.

368
00:23:34,999 --> 00:23:36,000
Don't you wanna remember?

369
00:23:36,625 --> 00:23:39,336
Why can't gods
speak in ways we understand?

370
00:23:42,047 --> 00:23:43,841
Who doesn't understand thunder?

371
00:23:44,633 --> 00:23:45,759
Or fire?

372
00:23:46,260 --> 00:23:48,971
A child's smile, a good harvest?

373
00:23:49,054 --> 00:23:51,140
But you hide yourselves from us.

374
00:23:52,850 --> 00:23:53,851
Look.

375
00:23:54,727 --> 00:23:56,145
Who's moving that cloud?

376
00:23:57,104 --> 00:23:58,647
Amassing those birds?

377
00:24:00,065 --> 00:24:01,484
Who's moving the water?

378
00:24:03,527 --> 00:24:04,820
Who were you talking to?

379
00:24:07,740 --> 00:24:09,158
Athena came to me again.

380
00:24:09,950 --> 00:24:11,744
She's telling me to remember.

381
00:24:13,454 --> 00:24:14,538
To leave.

382
00:24:14,622 --> 00:24:15,623
Why would you leave?

383
00:24:16,248 --> 00:24:18,334
We have everything we need.

384
00:24:18,918 --> 00:24:20,836
How long have I been here, Calypso?

385
00:24:25,257 --> 00:24:27,051
I have a home somewhere.

386
00:24:28,886 --> 00:24:29,970
There's someone.

387
00:24:30,596 --> 00:24:31,722
Out there.

388
00:24:32,431 --> 00:24:33,599
How do you know?

389
00:24:34,099 --> 00:24:35,309
I don't know.

390
00:24:37,895 --> 00:24:38,896
I feel.

391
00:24:55,079 --> 00:24:56,997
What we have here could last forever.

392
00:24:57,081 --> 00:24:58,415
Forever is for gods.

393
00:24:58,499 --> 00:24:59,750
Are you a god?

394
00:25:02,753 --> 00:25:03,754
You wanna remember,

395
00:25:03,837 --> 00:25:06,340
but what if remembering
destroys your happiness?

396
00:25:06,423 --> 00:25:07,925
Then it wasn't real.

397
00:25:15,975 --> 00:25:17,017
No.

398
00:25:17,101 --> 00:25:18,602
- No more.
- But I love it.

399
00:25:19,103 --> 00:25:20,980
Stop eating the lotus.

400
00:25:21,063 --> 00:25:23,065
Tell me what you remember.

401
00:25:24,692 --> 00:25:25,693
After Troy.

402
00:25:27,861 --> 00:25:28,862
Troy.

403
00:25:35,244 --> 00:25:36,537
We won the war.

404
00:25:37,121 --> 00:25:38,414
And then what?

405
00:26:44,063 --> 00:26:45,689
Ten years on this fucking beach.

406
00:26:51,236 --> 00:26:52,404
Let's go home.

407
00:26:53,822 --> 00:26:55,699
Remember who we leave here.

408
00:27:00,329 --> 00:27:01,830
Think we have enough provisions?

409
00:27:02,623 --> 00:27:04,208
We've taken enough from Troy.

410
00:27:05,209 --> 00:27:06,752
Another stop or two won't kill us.

411
00:27:08,462 --> 00:27:10,589
And... pull!

412
00:27:11,465 --> 00:27:13,717
And... pull!

413
00:27:14,635 --> 00:27:16,929
And... pull!

414
00:27:17,846 --> 00:27:19,014
And...

415
00:27:19,098 --> 00:27:20,307
Stow the oars.

416
00:27:20,391 --> 00:27:21,392
Rest!

417
00:27:21,475 --> 00:27:22,685
Rest!

418
00:27:22,768 --> 00:27:23,811
Stow the oars!

419
00:27:23,894 --> 00:27:25,479
Stow the oars!

420
00:27:30,401 --> 00:27:31,568
We'll raise the sail,

421
00:27:31,652 --> 00:27:32,653
follow the southerly winds.

422
00:27:32,736 --> 00:27:33,737
Yes, sir.

423
00:27:34,780 --> 00:27:37,157
Agamemnon's rowing west
on the known provisions route.

424
00:27:37,241 --> 00:27:38,659
Well, he's got more mouths to feed.

425
00:27:39,702 --> 00:27:41,036
We'll find places to land.

426
00:27:41,120 --> 00:27:42,413
Are you sure? It's a risk.

427
00:27:42,496 --> 00:27:44,248
I followed Agamemnon long enough.

428
00:27:45,165 --> 00:27:47,584
With a little luck and the right winds,

429
00:27:47,668 --> 00:27:49,420
we'll see some of the world,

430
00:27:49,503 --> 00:27:51,296
still be home before Agamemnon.

431
00:27:54,800 --> 00:27:55,843
Steady, boys!

432
00:29:01,158 --> 00:29:02,785
Just keep the bear on the right.

433
00:29:12,461 --> 00:29:13,462
Odysseus!

434
00:29:13,545 --> 00:29:14,922
Land!

435
00:29:18,133 --> 00:29:19,301
See?

436
00:29:19,384 --> 00:29:21,887
Lucky bastard.

437
00:29:21,970 --> 00:29:23,555
We're down to our last supplies.

438
00:29:23,639 --> 00:29:25,182
Assemble a landing party.

439
00:29:25,265 --> 00:29:27,518
Food and water. Enough for three weeks.

440
00:29:27,601 --> 00:29:28,811
What if they won't give it?

441
00:29:30,854 --> 00:29:32,731
How badly do you wanna get home?

442
00:29:33,357 --> 00:29:35,275
Get your weapons ready!

443
00:29:35,359 --> 00:29:36,527
- Begging...
- Move it!

444
00:29:36,610 --> 00:29:37,653
Borrowing...

445
00:29:38,529 --> 00:29:39,613
Plundering.

446
00:29:43,408 --> 00:29:46,453
Ask him why they abandoned their village
instead of welcoming us?

447
00:29:53,502 --> 00:29:55,170
He says they thought
we were the sea people.

448
00:29:55,254 --> 00:29:56,296
No.

449
00:29:56,380 --> 00:29:58,799
We're Greeks. From Ithaca.

450
00:29:59,716 --> 00:30:01,468
Maybe you heard of our victory at Troy.

451
00:30:02,219 --> 00:30:04,429
We're headed west on the southerly winds.

452
00:30:11,061 --> 00:30:12,479
He says if we go that way,

453
00:30:13,230 --> 00:30:14,940
we won't like the things we'll find.

454
00:30:16,358 --> 00:30:18,068
After years of war,

455
00:30:19,069 --> 00:30:22,948
no one could stand
between my men and home.

456
00:30:23,866 --> 00:30:25,117
Not even me.

457
00:30:26,451 --> 00:30:28,245
Then something happened.

458
00:30:44,011 --> 00:30:45,888
Chasing the escaping sun.

459
00:30:46,597 --> 00:30:47,764
Land!

460
00:30:49,308 --> 00:30:50,642
Land!

461
00:30:50,726 --> 00:30:51,727
There you go.

462
00:30:55,063 --> 00:30:56,773
Lucky bastard.

463
00:31:08,869 --> 00:31:10,537
No, no, no.

464
00:31:10,621 --> 00:31:12,956
Sheep come in flocks.

465
00:31:14,207 --> 00:31:15,208
Let's follow.

466
00:32:35,872 --> 00:32:37,457
Someone lives here.

467
00:32:38,750 --> 00:32:39,918
Yeah, they make cheese.

468
00:32:58,770 --> 00:32:59,938
Pretty good.

469
00:33:02,774 --> 00:33:04,026
There's a lot of it.

470
00:33:04,109 --> 00:33:05,318
We should leave.

471
00:33:06,069 --> 00:33:07,612
We brought nothing to trade with.

472
00:33:08,280 --> 00:33:09,698
Zeus' Law.

473
00:33:09,781 --> 00:33:11,867
I don't know if whoever lives
here has heard of Zeus' Law.

474
00:33:11,950 --> 00:33:13,285
Or Zeus for that matter.

475
00:33:13,368 --> 00:33:15,162
Everyone's heard of Zeus' Law.

476
00:33:15,662 --> 00:33:16,872
Get the fire going!

477
00:33:16,955 --> 00:33:18,623
We'll welcome the owner back.

478
00:34:37,994 --> 00:34:39,162
Get away from the fire.

479
00:34:44,000 --> 00:34:45,585
Spread out!

480
00:36:18,553 --> 00:36:19,971
I think it's asleep.

481
00:37:05,308 --> 00:37:06,559
Wait.

482
00:37:09,562 --> 00:37:10,689
The rock.

483
00:37:18,321 --> 00:37:19,322
We can't move the rock.

484
00:37:19,406 --> 00:37:20,782
We'd be trapped.

485
00:37:29,249 --> 00:37:30,250
Spread the word.

486
00:37:30,333 --> 00:37:33,837
In the morning,
when it lets out its flock,

487
00:37:33,920 --> 00:37:35,380
we'll rush the entrance.

488
00:37:35,463 --> 00:37:36,589
Rest up.

489
00:38:01,573 --> 00:38:03,241
Go, go, go!

490
00:38:13,585 --> 00:38:14,961
Go!

491
00:38:23,261 --> 00:38:26,056
Could we jam something in the cave mouth?

492
00:38:26,139 --> 00:38:27,349
Leave a gap?

493
00:38:28,058 --> 00:38:29,059
Maybe.

494
00:38:30,101 --> 00:38:31,102
But what?

495
00:38:31,603 --> 00:38:32,729
This log.

496
00:38:36,399 --> 00:38:37,734
It'll notice.

497
00:38:39,319 --> 00:38:41,946
But grab the thin end. Put it in the fire.

498
00:38:42,822 --> 00:38:43,865
How much rope do we have?

499
00:38:44,991 --> 00:38:47,202
- How big's the flock?
- Couple dozen, maybe.

500
00:38:49,913 --> 00:38:51,206
There's too many of us.

501
00:38:51,706 --> 00:38:54,125
When it comes back,
two of us have to get out.

502
00:38:54,209 --> 00:38:55,710
Or at least get killed.

503
00:39:00,840 --> 00:39:02,801
Run low through the flock
towards the entrance,

504
00:39:02,884 --> 00:39:03,968
then split wide.

505
00:39:04,052 --> 00:39:06,763
- So he can only grab one of 'em.
- Right.

506
00:39:06,846 --> 00:39:09,349
Four of us should make the run.
Two and two.

507
00:39:09,432 --> 00:39:10,975
Why risk more men?

508
00:39:11,059 --> 00:39:12,519
It ate two of us last night.

509
00:39:12,602 --> 00:39:14,396
He'll want another two before he sleeps.

510
00:39:39,254 --> 00:39:40,296
Go, go, go!

511
00:41:18,853 --> 00:41:21,231
- What about his body?
- What about it?

512
00:41:21,314 --> 00:41:23,149
We just leave him here to rot?

513
00:41:24,442 --> 00:41:25,902
As food for that thing?

514
00:41:27,737 --> 00:41:30,448
We honor our dead, Odysseus.

515
00:41:30,532 --> 00:41:33,409
He died helping us escape.
We'll honor him by escaping.

516
00:41:36,829 --> 00:41:39,916
Father Poseidon, they blinded me.

517
00:41:40,833 --> 00:41:44,379
Vengeance, Father. Vengeance for pain.

518
00:41:44,462 --> 00:41:47,757
Poseidon, vengeance for pain.

519
00:41:47,840 --> 00:41:49,717
He can talk.

520
00:41:50,385 --> 00:41:51,719
Why didn't it talk before?

521
00:41:53,471 --> 00:41:54,931
Do you talk to ants?

522
00:41:56,474 --> 00:41:58,017
Should we reason with it?

523
00:42:00,520 --> 00:42:01,854
I think we're past that.

524
00:43:13,134 --> 00:43:14,469
What are you doing?

525
00:43:15,386 --> 00:43:17,555
No!

526
00:43:33,988 --> 00:43:36,199
Come on. Go, go!

527
00:43:36,282 --> 00:43:38,117
Go!

528
00:44:06,187 --> 00:44:07,188
Get in!

529
00:44:09,399 --> 00:44:10,400
Go!

530
00:44:31,713 --> 00:44:33,172
- Go, go, go!
- Go, go, go!

531
00:44:34,465 --> 00:44:37,135
Go! Go!

532
00:44:57,780 --> 00:44:59,574
Why'd you
have to fire that arrow?

533
00:44:59,657 --> 00:45:01,826
We lost good soldiers to that beast.

534
00:45:01,909 --> 00:45:04,954
Yeah, and that beast
was the son of Poseidon.

535
00:45:05,037 --> 00:45:06,122
What?

536
00:45:06,205 --> 00:45:08,750
When we put its eye out,
we heard it crying his name.

537
00:45:08,833 --> 00:45:10,543
That thing was a monster.

538
00:45:10,626 --> 00:45:12,462
I heard it too.

539
00:45:12,545 --> 00:45:14,547
So now the sea's against us?

540
00:45:14,630 --> 00:45:16,591
And the wind.

541
00:45:16,674 --> 00:45:19,260
How are we supposed to get
home with Poseidon against us?

542
00:45:19,343 --> 00:45:21,846
We can't live by omens and sacrifices.

543
00:45:24,098 --> 00:45:26,100
Do you know how many times
I've heard you weeping,

544
00:45:26,184 --> 00:45:28,269
praying to Athena since we left Troy?

545
00:45:28,352 --> 00:45:29,812
Couldn't you have shown some mercy?

546
00:45:31,272 --> 00:45:32,273
Take this.

547
00:45:34,859 --> 00:45:35,985
Wear it.

548
00:45:36,068 --> 00:45:38,321
To remember those we couldn't bury.

549
00:45:40,072 --> 00:45:41,908
The gods help those who help themselves.

550
00:45:45,661 --> 00:45:49,332
From that moment,
every rough sea or headwind

551
00:45:49,582 --> 00:45:51,167
told the crew we were cursed.

552
00:45:53,586 --> 00:45:55,171
But where is my crew?

553
00:45:56,798 --> 00:45:58,716
When will you let me see my men?

554
00:45:59,717 --> 00:46:02,261
If I could just talk to them
they'd help me remember.

555
00:46:02,345 --> 00:46:03,346
Did I have a wife?

556
00:46:03,429 --> 00:46:04,764
Children? Maybe a son.

557
00:46:04,847 --> 00:46:06,849
How long have I been here?

558
00:46:08,476 --> 00:46:09,519
A long time.

559
00:46:10,102 --> 00:46:11,103
If I had a son,

560
00:46:13,773 --> 00:46:15,274
how old would he be now?

561
00:46:17,318 --> 00:46:19,320
I don't know how to speak to a king.

562
00:46:19,403 --> 00:46:20,738
You'll do fine.

563
00:46:20,822 --> 00:46:22,114
Just keep your mouth shut.

564
00:46:22,907 --> 00:46:26,369
But, if anyone asks,
we're traders from Crete.

565
00:46:26,452 --> 00:46:28,579
Then we'll figure out
how to approach the king.

566
00:46:38,464 --> 00:46:39,715
Welcome.

567
00:46:39,799 --> 00:46:41,843
Thank you. You seem to be having quite...

568
00:46:41,926 --> 00:46:43,135
This way.

569
00:46:48,307 --> 00:46:49,559
Where are you taking us?

570
00:46:49,642 --> 00:46:52,311
The king insists
on meeting strangers himself.

571
00:47:13,666 --> 00:47:14,667
Sit.

572
00:47:18,212 --> 00:47:19,213
Not you.

573
00:47:20,590 --> 00:47:21,591
You.

574
00:47:24,010 --> 00:47:25,928
Enjoy our hospitality.

575
00:47:26,012 --> 00:47:27,471
As Zeus demands.

576
00:47:28,014 --> 00:47:29,849
Then, perhaps, you'll tell us who you are.

577
00:47:31,225 --> 00:47:32,852
You picked the right day to arrive.

578
00:47:32,935 --> 00:47:35,062
Feast of my daughter's wedding.

579
00:47:35,146 --> 00:47:37,648
Tomorrow she leaves to start a new life.

580
00:47:38,441 --> 00:47:40,526
You don't look like a beggar.

581
00:47:40,610 --> 00:47:41,736
Imagine you know who I am.

582
00:47:43,821 --> 00:47:46,657
I'm Menelaus. The son of Atreus.

583
00:47:47,158 --> 00:47:48,910
Brother of Agamemnon.

584
00:47:49,744 --> 00:47:51,329
The husband of Helen.

585
00:47:51,913 --> 00:47:52,955
Perhaps you've heard of Helen.

586
00:47:56,000 --> 00:47:59,629
Face that launched a thousand ships.

587
00:48:01,422 --> 00:48:03,299
Though maybe now just 500.

588
00:48:07,929 --> 00:48:10,932
So many left on the battlefields of Troy
for that face.

589
00:48:11,015 --> 00:48:13,225
For your brother's ambition.

590
00:48:14,143 --> 00:48:15,978
This one looks familiar.

591
00:48:17,396 --> 00:48:18,481
Ajax...

592
00:48:19,106 --> 00:48:20,149
Patroclus...

593
00:48:20,232 --> 00:48:21,609
Achilles...

594
00:48:22,151 --> 00:48:25,696
I don't miss them as much
as one particular warrior.

595
00:48:26,530 --> 00:48:28,199
He was the smartest of us all.

596
00:48:30,660 --> 00:48:32,954
Ten years we rotted on that beach.

597
00:48:46,342 --> 00:48:47,718
Deadlocked.

598
00:48:47,802 --> 00:48:49,804
Fighting amongst ourselves until one man...

599
00:48:53,140 --> 00:48:54,475
He found a way.

600
00:48:56,227 --> 00:48:57,478
Odysseus.

601
00:48:58,562 --> 00:48:59,772
You hear the story of the horse?

602
00:49:03,526 --> 00:49:04,777
But did you hear it from the inside?

603
00:49:09,657 --> 00:49:10,866
We were in there for days.

604
00:49:12,284 --> 00:49:14,870
We squeezed inside
before the ship set sail.

605
00:49:16,330 --> 00:49:18,791
Odysseus, he knew that no one could know
that we were inside.

606
00:49:18,874 --> 00:49:21,669
Not even Sinon,
the soldier we left behind.

607
00:49:27,133 --> 00:49:30,011
First two tides, our men
drowned right in front of us.

608
00:49:35,766 --> 00:49:38,561
We lay silent.
In our piss and shit.

609
00:50:06,881 --> 00:50:08,049
A gift.

610
00:50:08,674 --> 00:50:10,051
For Athena.

611
00:50:12,386 --> 00:50:13,554
Common gods.

612
00:50:14,346 --> 00:50:15,890
Respect their offering.

613
00:51:37,429 --> 00:51:39,557
You should be burned!

614
00:51:39,640 --> 00:51:41,851
Burn it! Burn it!

615
00:52:03,956 --> 00:52:05,332
It's an offering for Athena!

616
00:52:14,425 --> 00:52:16,677
It's an offering for Athena!

617
00:54:52,666 --> 00:54:53,709
The gates!

618
00:54:55,586 --> 00:54:57,671
He knew to wait
for a moonless night

619
00:54:57,755 --> 00:54:59,381
on the plains of Troy.

620
00:55:06,055 --> 00:55:07,389
Go! Go!

621
00:55:12,644 --> 00:55:14,855
- City's under attack!
- Get to the gates!

622
00:55:15,439 --> 00:55:16,648
To the gates!

623
00:55:35,667 --> 00:55:36,960
Go, go!

624
00:55:37,920 --> 00:55:39,713
The city's under attack!

625
00:56:51,452 --> 00:56:53,412
- Come on!
- Go, go!

626
00:57:26,570 --> 00:57:28,280
I think you know the rest.

627
00:57:28,363 --> 00:57:29,364
You moved by my telling it?

628
00:57:29,448 --> 00:57:31,617
You should hear the bards sing it.

629
00:57:31,700 --> 00:57:32,826
I have.

630
00:57:36,246 --> 00:57:37,789
But they weren't there with all of you.

631
00:57:37,873 --> 00:57:39,625
With your father.

632
00:57:39,708 --> 00:57:41,043
Odysseus.

633
00:57:43,795 --> 00:57:45,047
You're so like him.

634
00:57:45,923 --> 00:57:47,508
When did you last see him?

635
00:57:47,591 --> 00:57:48,842
Troy.

636
00:57:49,593 --> 00:57:51,762
The gods granted us a righteous victory...

637
00:57:51,845 --> 00:57:52,846
Righteous.

638
00:57:53,847 --> 00:57:57,684
The gods granted our righteous victory
but did not bless our voyages.

639
00:57:58,810 --> 00:58:00,854
Took me years to get home.

640
00:58:00,938 --> 00:58:01,939
But he didn't.

641
00:58:02,022 --> 00:58:03,565
Some did and wished they hadn't.

642
00:58:03,649 --> 00:58:05,901
Tell him.

643
00:58:05,984 --> 00:58:08,654
- Tell him about your brother.
- No.

644
00:58:08,737 --> 00:58:10,072
About the fate
that the gods bestowed on him

645
00:58:10,155 --> 00:58:11,657
after your righteous struggle.

646
00:58:11,740 --> 00:58:13,408
- No.
- About his hero's welcome.

647
00:58:13,492 --> 00:58:14,826
- No.
- About how Agamemnon

648
00:58:14,910 --> 00:58:16,870
- made it home years before us.
- No.

649
00:58:16,954 --> 00:58:19,081
Tell him how he was
welcomed by his queen,

650
00:58:19,164 --> 00:58:20,916
my twin sister Clytemnestra.

651
00:58:20,999 --> 00:58:22,417
- No. No.
- Tell him how she...

652
00:58:22,501 --> 00:58:24,920
No! He does not want to hear
about my brother!

653
00:58:28,173 --> 00:58:29,466
He wants news of his father.

654
00:58:30,634 --> 00:58:33,011
I know nothing of Odysseus since Troy.

655
00:58:33,095 --> 00:58:34,638
Nothing?

656
00:58:35,514 --> 00:58:37,307
Not from any of your men?

657
00:58:37,975 --> 00:58:40,477
Or Agamemnon's, or traders,
or travelers that told you something?

658
00:58:40,561 --> 00:58:43,689
Ah, did you come here for rumors?
Is that what you're interested in? Gossip?

659
00:58:44,398 --> 00:58:45,774
Well, you're in the right place.

660
00:58:45,857 --> 00:58:48,777
Anyone wanna spin a story about Odysseus?

661
00:58:49,278 --> 00:58:52,114
Some say he's rich or some say poor.

662
00:58:53,156 --> 00:58:55,617
Some say he perished.
Some say he's in prison.

663
00:58:55,701 --> 00:58:57,244
- What say you?
- In prison?

664
00:59:00,539 --> 00:59:01,873
What kind of prison

665
00:59:02,791 --> 00:59:04,418
could hold a man like that?

666
00:59:05,627 --> 00:59:07,796
Tell me what you do remember.

667
00:59:07,879 --> 00:59:08,964
The rest will come.

668
00:59:10,215 --> 00:59:13,885
The men became more and more convinced
that I'd offended Poseidon.

669
00:59:34,448 --> 00:59:36,533
- Ready!
- Ready!

670
00:59:37,868 --> 00:59:39,286
Pull!

671
00:59:39,870 --> 00:59:42,873
And... pull!

672
00:59:44,333 --> 00:59:46,168
And... pull!

673
00:59:57,804 --> 00:59:59,723
Pull! And...

674
00:59:59,806 --> 01:00:01,683
We were hit by storm after storm.

675
01:00:01,767 --> 01:00:03,935
All pushing us off course.

676
01:00:04,019 --> 01:00:06,021
Until we were truly lost.

677
01:00:09,733 --> 01:00:12,277
And... pull!

678
01:00:12,361 --> 01:00:14,571
And... pull!

679
01:00:17,032 --> 01:00:18,033
Pull!

680
01:00:18,909 --> 01:00:22,663
And... pull!

681
01:00:22,746 --> 01:00:24,998
And... pull!

682
01:00:58,448 --> 01:00:59,533
Hello.

683
01:01:01,451 --> 01:01:02,494
We're here in peace.

684
01:01:05,247 --> 01:01:06,957
We just need some food.

685
01:01:12,879 --> 01:01:14,506
We need food.

686
01:01:15,882 --> 01:01:17,968
- Can you understand that?
- It's just a child.

687
01:01:20,554 --> 01:01:23,223
If that's a child, what about its parents?

688
01:01:25,308 --> 01:01:27,227
Hey. Shh! It's okay.

689
01:01:27,894 --> 01:01:30,647
No, no, no.

690
01:01:49,082 --> 01:01:51,126
Go! Go!

691
01:01:51,209 --> 01:01:53,503
Back to the ship! Go!

692
01:02:08,101 --> 01:02:09,269
Go, go. Go.

693
01:02:18,236 --> 01:02:20,238
Fall back! Fall back! Go! Go!

694
01:03:30,225 --> 01:03:31,810
Run! Faster!

695
01:03:41,111 --> 01:03:43,405
Get my bow! My bow!

696
01:03:47,784 --> 01:03:48,910
Let's go!

697
01:03:58,336 --> 01:04:00,255
Let's go! Come on!

698
01:04:00,338 --> 01:04:01,756
And... pull!

699
01:04:01,840 --> 01:04:03,300
Wait!

700
01:04:03,884 --> 01:04:05,468
And... pull!

701
01:04:05,552 --> 01:04:06,887
Wait!

702
01:04:06,970 --> 01:04:08,430
And... pull!

703
01:04:09,014 --> 01:04:11,224
And... pull!

704
01:04:12,017 --> 01:04:14,019
And... pull!

705
01:04:14,102 --> 01:04:15,145
Go!

706
01:04:15,228 --> 01:04:17,522
- Wait!
- And... pull!

707
01:04:18,773 --> 01:04:19,608
Wait!

708
01:04:30,619 --> 01:04:32,037
Push the oars!

709
01:04:37,876 --> 01:04:41,046
All right! Move this bastard!

710
01:05:34,766 --> 01:05:35,850
Turn to port.

711
01:05:35,934 --> 01:05:37,519
Follow the coast.

712
01:05:37,602 --> 01:05:39,104
What if they have ships?

713
01:05:39,187 --> 01:05:41,064
We need to get as far away as possible.

714
01:05:46,027 --> 01:05:48,279
We don't have supplies
for a blind crossing.

715
01:05:48,989 --> 01:05:50,782
We've lost our way.

716
01:05:50,865 --> 01:05:53,952
We need to find food and get our bearings.

717
01:05:56,579 --> 01:05:57,580
Turn to port.

718
01:06:24,315 --> 01:06:27,318
There! Smoke! There!

719
01:06:27,402 --> 01:06:30,321
Go past, we'll find anchorage
at the beach!

720
01:06:37,370 --> 01:06:38,830
Oars up!

721
01:06:42,542 --> 01:06:44,669
Form a landing party.

722
01:06:46,463 --> 01:06:48,048
Tomorrow.

723
01:06:50,341 --> 01:06:51,342
Tomorrow.

724
01:07:01,936 --> 01:07:03,646
Leave someone with the tenders.

725
01:07:04,606 --> 01:07:06,066
We think you should stay.

726
01:07:08,693 --> 01:07:10,361
- "We"?
- All of us.

727
01:07:11,029 --> 01:07:12,238
Still alive.

728
01:07:23,041 --> 01:07:24,417
Is this a mutiny?

729
01:07:24,501 --> 01:07:25,877
That's up to you.

730
01:07:30,006 --> 01:07:31,007
Give me your bow.

731
01:07:31,091 --> 01:07:32,926
I'll make sure we eat tonight.

732
01:07:33,510 --> 01:07:34,886
No matter what you find.

733
01:09:21,492 --> 01:09:23,912
No, no! It's okay! It's okay!

734
01:09:27,874 --> 01:09:29,000
- Please don't hurt me!
- It's okay.

735
01:09:29,083 --> 01:09:30,418
- You're okay! It's all right!
- No!

736
01:09:30,501 --> 01:09:31,502
No one's gonna hurt you!

737
01:09:31,586 --> 01:09:33,296
If I do what you want.

738
01:09:33,379 --> 01:09:35,632
No one is going to hurt you.

739
01:09:39,427 --> 01:09:41,346
My name's Eurylochus. What's your name?

740
01:09:41,429 --> 01:09:42,513
Circe.

741
01:09:42,597 --> 01:09:45,475
Circe, we need food. Badly.

742
01:09:46,601 --> 01:09:47,894
I have food.

743
01:09:49,562 --> 01:09:50,980
Lots of food.

744
01:10:12,377 --> 01:10:13,461
Here, let me.

745
01:10:16,589 --> 01:10:18,508
Your weapons, your armor,

746
01:10:19,968 --> 01:10:21,052
put it out the back.

747
01:11:04,220 --> 01:11:05,847
You like my meat?

748
01:11:06,431 --> 01:11:08,933
Why... do you have so much?

749
01:11:09,767 --> 01:11:12,270
You never know when strangers will come.

750
01:11:22,739 --> 01:11:23,740
Men.

751
01:11:26,200 --> 01:11:27,910
With their appetites.

752
01:11:29,495 --> 01:11:31,372
Too much is never enough.

753
01:12:47,281 --> 01:12:48,866
Eurylochus!

754
01:13:14,475 --> 01:13:15,768
They haven't come back yet.

755
01:13:15,852 --> 01:13:16,978
- None of them?
- No.

756
01:13:17,061 --> 01:13:18,229
Give me your dagger.

757
01:13:21,941 --> 01:13:23,025
Stay with the boat!

758
01:14:04,442 --> 01:14:05,902
Why don't you just come here?

759
01:14:14,285 --> 01:14:16,037
Do you come from the sea?

760
01:14:17,872 --> 01:14:19,207
Are you alone?

761
01:14:24,295 --> 01:14:26,506
My pigs have taken a liking to you.

762
01:14:27,298 --> 01:14:28,674
Are you hungry?

763
01:14:30,801 --> 01:14:32,637
Leave your weapons at the door.

764
01:14:32,720 --> 01:14:35,223
Enjoy the hospitality of Zeus.

765
01:14:36,599 --> 01:14:39,227
Grab a hog. You can
slaughter it for your dinner.

766
01:15:04,627 --> 01:15:07,255
Whoever you are,
you're clearly not a farmer.

767
01:15:07,338 --> 01:15:08,339
Here.

768
01:15:12,176 --> 01:15:13,636
That stew smells good.

769
01:15:17,265 --> 01:15:19,016
That's enough for just us.

770
01:15:20,059 --> 01:15:21,644
Who says it's just us?

771
01:15:23,145 --> 01:15:24,522
Who else is here?

772
01:15:25,106 --> 01:15:27,233
Who indeed?

773
01:15:39,328 --> 01:15:40,788
You say you're all alone.

774
01:15:41,622 --> 01:15:44,125
But no one comes here alone.

775
01:15:44,208 --> 01:15:45,585
No?

776
01:15:45,668 --> 01:15:48,129
You can only reach this spot by sea.

777
01:15:48,713 --> 01:15:51,132
So where are your shipmates?

778
01:15:52,008 --> 01:15:53,426
I'm looking for them.

779
01:15:54,343 --> 01:15:55,636
Anyone happen to come this way?

780
01:15:59,140 --> 01:16:00,725
But you prepared all this food?

781
01:16:00,808 --> 01:16:03,185
You're questioning me? You?

782
01:16:05,313 --> 01:16:07,481
A traveler who's clearly starving,

783
01:16:07,565 --> 01:16:09,817
yet hasn't touched the food
in front of him.

784
01:16:09,900 --> 01:16:13,446
Claims to be a sailor,
yet wears a great warrior's armor.

785
01:16:14,447 --> 01:16:16,240
And who breaks Zeus' Law

786
01:16:16,824 --> 01:16:19,744
by bringing a dagger
into his hostess' house.

787
01:16:19,827 --> 01:16:21,120
You have sharp eyes.

788
01:16:21,203 --> 01:16:23,706
Sharpened by the gods.

789
01:16:23,789 --> 01:16:25,708
Do they grant you insights?

790
01:16:25,791 --> 01:16:27,168
Truths.

791
01:16:28,336 --> 01:16:29,754
Sometimes.

792
01:16:29,837 --> 01:16:31,881
Would they tell you
where I could find my men?

793
01:16:32,840 --> 01:16:33,966
Your men?

794
01:16:34,050 --> 01:16:35,176
That's right.

795
01:16:35,801 --> 01:16:37,136
They would.

796
01:16:37,219 --> 01:16:38,346
And would you tell me?

797
01:16:38,971 --> 01:16:40,264
Perhaps.

798
01:16:41,974 --> 01:16:43,476
If you give me your dagger.

799
01:16:52,193 --> 01:16:55,821
But why would I help a liar
who refuses my hospitality?

800
01:17:06,332 --> 01:17:09,168
Your men were here earlier,
and now they're not.

801
01:17:15,841 --> 01:17:16,842
They moved on?

802
01:17:17,468 --> 01:17:18,928
Yes.

803
01:17:21,347 --> 01:17:22,973
Leaving their rings?

804
01:17:30,648 --> 01:17:31,649
I wouldn't.

805
01:17:33,442 --> 01:17:34,318
Don't.

806
01:17:34,402 --> 01:17:36,153
I don't know who this crow is to you,

807
01:17:36,237 --> 01:17:39,281
but if you don't put that knife down,
I will throttle it to find out.

808
01:17:39,365 --> 01:17:40,658
Don't hurt her.

809
01:17:40,741 --> 01:17:41,951
Family?

810
01:17:43,828 --> 01:17:45,162
Mother?

811
01:17:47,373 --> 01:17:48,374
Your sister.

812
01:17:52,044 --> 01:17:54,755
You did this to her like
you turned my men into pigs.

813
01:17:54,839 --> 01:17:56,382
I didn't turn your men into anything.

814
01:17:57,341 --> 01:18:00,052
Look at them. This is who they are.
Look at them!

815
01:18:00,636 --> 01:18:03,013
Base. Disgusting.

816
01:18:03,097 --> 01:18:05,641
Primal urges that you value in battle.

817
01:18:05,725 --> 01:18:08,310
They've raped and pillaged
across the world for you.

818
01:18:08,394 --> 01:18:10,187
And you think that's against their nature?

819
01:18:10,271 --> 01:18:12,523
You think they only did that
because you ordered them to?

820
01:18:12,606 --> 01:18:16,610
A true commander thinks his men's savagery
is actually discipline.

821
01:18:17,361 --> 01:18:19,447
But you don't seem that deluded.

822
01:18:19,530 --> 01:18:21,323
But you sent them to me here.

823
01:18:21,407 --> 01:18:22,950
On my own.

824
01:18:23,033 --> 01:18:26,912
A gang of filthy soldiers
with empty bellies and hot blood!

825
01:18:28,205 --> 01:18:31,834
You thought
they'd respect me in my home?

826
01:18:32,585 --> 01:18:33,836
Look at them!

827
01:18:33,919 --> 01:18:34,962
Know your own men.

828
01:18:36,756 --> 01:18:38,007
Did they hurt you?

829
01:18:38,090 --> 01:18:39,425
They would've.

830
01:18:40,009 --> 01:18:41,761
They just wanna go home.

831
01:18:45,139 --> 01:18:46,265
Let them.

832
01:19:12,458 --> 01:19:13,876
Come back.

833
01:19:14,919 --> 01:19:16,378
Come back.

834
01:19:17,213 --> 01:19:18,631
Come back.

835
01:19:18,714 --> 01:19:20,716
Back into your disguises.

836
01:19:24,929 --> 01:19:26,388
Come back.

837
01:19:26,472 --> 01:19:28,849
Back into your disguises.

838
01:19:28,933 --> 01:19:30,643
Come back.

839
01:19:30,726 --> 01:19:32,478
Back into your disguises.

840
01:19:33,020 --> 01:19:35,523
Come back. Come back.

841
01:19:36,607 --> 01:19:37,817
Come back.

842
01:19:37,900 --> 01:19:40,444
Back into your disguises.

843
01:19:40,528 --> 01:19:42,154
Come back.

844
01:19:45,825 --> 01:19:47,284
Odysseus.

845
01:19:47,368 --> 01:19:48,828
Back to the ship!

846
01:19:48,911 --> 01:19:50,621
Take all the food you can carry.

847
01:19:50,704 --> 01:19:52,331
Leave the meat.

848
01:19:52,414 --> 01:19:54,875
You're not gonna let her live.
She's a witch.

849
01:19:54,959 --> 01:19:56,460
Who gave her powers?

850
01:19:56,544 --> 01:19:58,128
Your gods.

851
01:19:58,212 --> 01:20:00,172
Fill the jugs and skins at the stream.

852
01:20:00,256 --> 01:20:01,924
Be ready to sail at dawn.

853
01:20:07,471 --> 01:20:09,139
Why leave her like this?

854
01:20:09,723 --> 01:20:11,350
We get on better this way.

855
01:20:12,810 --> 01:20:14,645
I'm sorry if we offended you.

856
01:20:15,729 --> 01:20:17,940
We can't get back without your help.

857
01:20:18,023 --> 01:20:20,150
We need to know the way
to the trade routes.

858
01:20:20,234 --> 01:20:21,944
You're too lost.

859
01:20:22,027 --> 01:20:23,070
What do you mean?

860
01:20:24,655 --> 01:20:27,408
Only blind Tiresias
can tell you how to go home.

861
01:20:27,491 --> 01:20:28,492
Tiresias?

862
01:20:28,576 --> 01:20:30,160
In Hades.

863
01:20:30,244 --> 01:20:33,247
Nobody sails into Hell. And comes back.

864
01:20:33,330 --> 01:20:34,415
You will.

865
01:20:35,958 --> 01:20:37,751
Head north. Into the twilight.

866
01:20:43,549 --> 01:20:45,467
Then head inland.

867
01:20:45,551 --> 01:20:48,596
Until the river of fire
meets a river of ice.

868
01:21:39,063 --> 01:21:40,606
There you must dig a trench.

869
01:21:43,776 --> 01:21:45,736
And fill it with the blood of sacrifice.

870
01:22:06,173 --> 01:22:07,549
Gather the dead.

871
01:22:08,801 --> 01:22:10,427
And learn how you go home.

872
01:22:12,513 --> 01:22:13,806
You.

873
01:22:14,848 --> 01:22:16,308
Not your men.

874
01:22:16,934 --> 01:22:18,560
You cursed them?

875
01:22:18,644 --> 01:22:19,853
Not a curse.

876
01:22:21,063 --> 01:22:22,272
A truth.

877
01:22:23,148 --> 01:22:25,943
Your men don't want you to defy the gods.

878
01:22:27,528 --> 01:22:29,780
But the gods say only you make it home.

879
01:22:30,864 --> 01:22:32,658
Then I defy the gods.

880
01:23:14,116 --> 01:23:15,159
Sinon.

881
01:23:15,826 --> 01:23:16,994
It's Odysseus.

882
01:23:21,707 --> 01:23:22,708
Sinon.

883
01:23:23,876 --> 01:23:24,877
It's Odysseus.

884
01:23:27,671 --> 01:23:29,381
Let him drink, Odysseus.

885
01:23:30,215 --> 01:23:32,051
Then you'll hear his testament.

886
01:23:46,523 --> 01:23:47,691
Odysseus?

887
01:23:47,775 --> 01:23:48,942
Yes.

888
01:23:49,026 --> 01:23:51,070
You lied to everyone.

889
01:23:54,948 --> 01:23:56,825
Did you have to lie to me?

890
01:23:57,785 --> 01:23:59,453
I needed you to believe.

891
01:24:01,413 --> 01:24:03,415
Why did you have to volunteer?

892
01:24:03,499 --> 01:24:05,292
Don't you remember how I came here?

893
01:24:05,375 --> 01:24:08,295
I begged to take Antinous' place.

894
01:24:08,378 --> 01:24:09,963
He'd been called.

895
01:24:10,672 --> 01:24:14,009
He offered money for your family
if you'd take his place.

896
01:24:14,593 --> 01:24:16,637
If the Trojans don't let me live,

897
01:24:19,014 --> 01:24:20,474
if I never make it home,

898
01:24:20,557 --> 01:24:22,810
take this back to my father.

899
01:24:23,894 --> 01:24:25,521
Tell him everything I did.

900
01:24:28,565 --> 01:24:30,067
Odysseus.

901
01:24:31,401 --> 01:24:33,904
Feel free to add some stuff.

902
01:24:37,074 --> 01:24:39,493
You didn't trust me with the truth?

903
01:24:41,203 --> 01:24:43,330
You knew I'd die for you.

904
01:24:43,413 --> 01:24:46,291
Dying men tell the truth.
I needed you to believe.

905
01:24:46,375 --> 01:24:49,169
I died for your lie on that beach.

906
01:24:49,920 --> 01:24:52,005
I know. I was there with you.

907
01:24:52,714 --> 01:24:55,634
I heard you repeat the lie
with your dying breath.

908
01:24:55,717 --> 01:24:57,052
Winning us the war.

909
01:24:57,136 --> 01:24:58,846
I was right there. Inches from you.

910
01:24:58,929 --> 01:24:59,972
Smelling your blood.

911
01:25:00,055 --> 01:25:02,057
What comfort could that give me?

912
01:25:03,475 --> 01:25:07,437
Not knowing you were inside that horse
until the dead told me.

913
01:25:08,438 --> 01:25:09,648
Which dead?

914
01:25:09,731 --> 01:25:13,360
A lot of veterans of your campaigns
walk these shores.

915
01:25:15,571 --> 01:25:20,826
The dead meet to lament the living
and greet the newly dead.

916
01:25:20,909 --> 01:25:23,328
My father was the first shade to find me.

917
01:25:24,037 --> 01:25:25,497
To comfort you.

918
01:25:25,581 --> 01:25:27,040
Shades don't comfort.

919
01:25:27,749 --> 01:25:30,919
They want word of the living
who wronged them.

920
01:25:31,795 --> 01:25:34,298
My father had died in poverty,

921
01:25:34,381 --> 01:25:39,344
alone, begging for scraps from people
like Antinous.

922
01:25:39,428 --> 01:25:41,221
Antinous cheated you?

923
01:25:41,847 --> 01:25:43,974
As he's no doubt cheating you.

924
01:25:44,057 --> 01:25:44,892
How?

925
01:25:44,975 --> 01:25:47,436
Who's in Ithaca looking after
your wife and son?

926
01:25:47,519 --> 01:25:49,980
My wife is of no concern.

927
01:25:51,190 --> 01:25:53,692
Talk to Agamemnon about homecomings.

928
01:25:54,318 --> 01:25:55,736
Wait. Agamemnon's here?

929
01:25:59,114 --> 01:26:01,408
You buried me at Troy with respect.

930
01:26:01,491 --> 01:26:02,618
Thank you.

931
01:26:03,327 --> 01:26:05,162
But spare a thought for those you didn't.

932
01:26:05,746 --> 01:26:07,706
It's not always possible.

933
01:26:08,290 --> 01:26:11,418
When you get home, take a ship.

934
01:26:11,501 --> 01:26:14,713
Head into the unknown west
till you find land.

935
01:26:14,796 --> 01:26:16,798
And there, give sacrifice

936
01:26:16,882 --> 01:26:19,009
and honor your men.

937
01:26:20,594 --> 01:26:22,721
Do you still have the lot?

938
01:26:25,891 --> 01:26:27,559
Of course.

939
01:26:27,643 --> 01:26:28,644
If you make it home,

940
01:26:28,727 --> 01:26:30,812
give Antinous back his shame.

941
01:26:30,896 --> 01:26:32,022
I will.

942
01:26:43,408 --> 01:26:44,451
My lord.

943
01:26:56,046 --> 01:26:57,339
Odysseus.

944
01:26:58,131 --> 01:26:59,341
How are you here?

945
01:26:59,883 --> 01:27:01,927
We won the war. You headed for home.

946
01:27:02,427 --> 01:27:06,014
Odysseus, the most brilliant
of all my generals.

947
01:27:07,307 --> 01:27:08,976
You would never be snared as I was.

948
01:27:09,059 --> 01:27:10,936
Were you cast adrift? Like us?

949
01:27:11,436 --> 01:27:12,437
No.

950
01:27:12,521 --> 01:27:14,523
My misfortune was to make it home.

951
01:27:15,691 --> 01:27:18,026
To be welcomed by my beautiful wife.

952
01:27:24,032 --> 01:27:25,158
Bathed.

953
01:27:27,661 --> 01:27:28,996
Anointed.

954
01:27:31,123 --> 01:27:32,416
Made soft.

955
01:27:33,208 --> 01:27:34,668
To learn the hardest lesson.

956
01:27:35,752 --> 01:27:38,588
That I'd left a poison
in her heart years before.

957
01:27:41,258 --> 01:27:42,634
Good part of her heart
had gone to another.

958
01:27:45,929 --> 01:27:48,390
No! No.

959
01:27:49,266 --> 01:27:51,893
She and her lover had
plotted my murder for years.

960
01:27:53,353 --> 01:27:54,396
Odysseus.

961
01:27:55,522 --> 01:27:56,732
Yes.

962
01:27:56,815 --> 01:28:00,319
My son Orestes, did he avenge me?

963
01:28:00,402 --> 01:28:01,695
My lord, how could I know?

964
01:28:02,821 --> 01:28:04,448
I'm still trying to get home.

965
01:28:05,574 --> 01:28:07,075
Don't make my mistake.

966
01:28:08,201 --> 01:28:11,330
Don't walk in the front door
expecting garlands and praise.

967
01:28:12,039 --> 01:28:13,290
Come in disguise.

968
01:28:14,207 --> 01:28:15,250
Take your time.

969
01:28:16,126 --> 01:28:17,252
Assess.

970
01:28:24,051 --> 01:28:25,510
Do you see?

971
01:28:27,346 --> 01:28:28,805
I know what I have to do.

972
01:28:28,889 --> 01:28:30,599
Some of what you have to do.

973
01:28:30,682 --> 01:28:33,268
My prophecy is not for their ears.

974
01:28:34,478 --> 01:28:35,645
Back to the ship.

975
01:28:37,731 --> 01:28:38,815
Sail southwest.

976
01:28:38,899 --> 01:28:40,650
Past the sirens.

977
01:28:41,735 --> 01:28:43,987
Their song lures your men onto the rocks.

978
01:28:44,071 --> 01:28:45,072
They all die.

979
01:28:45,155 --> 01:28:46,615
I won't let that happen.

980
01:28:47,157 --> 01:28:49,618
I'll block their ears. With wax.

981
01:28:49,701 --> 01:28:50,744
Past the sirens

982
01:28:50,827 --> 01:28:53,372
you choose between the whirlpool Charybdis

983
01:28:53,455 --> 01:28:54,748
and the monster Scylla.

984
01:28:54,831 --> 01:28:57,542
The whirlpool in the sea
takes all of your lives.

985
01:28:57,626 --> 01:28:59,920
The monster in the cliffs, only six.

986
01:29:00,003 --> 01:29:01,671
But your determination

987
01:29:01,755 --> 01:29:04,216
to not sacrifice any more
lives kills all of them.

988
01:29:05,425 --> 01:29:06,468
I'll do what I have to.

989
01:29:06,551 --> 01:29:09,388
At the end, when starvation
and mutiny threaten,

990
01:29:09,471 --> 01:29:13,141
they demand to land where
the Sun God grazes his cattle.

991
01:29:13,767 --> 01:29:15,477
They slaughter Apollo's herd.

992
01:29:16,728 --> 01:29:18,355
Zeus kills them for it.

993
01:29:18,438 --> 01:29:19,689
Do you see?

994
01:29:20,816 --> 01:29:22,150
I'll stop them.

995
01:29:22,234 --> 01:29:25,529
Why do you value these men's lives
more than those you've already lost?

996
01:29:25,612 --> 01:29:27,656
I can still save them from the gods.

997
01:29:27,739 --> 01:29:30,158
But not from themselves.

998
01:29:33,161 --> 01:29:34,204
What is that?

999
01:29:34,287 --> 01:29:35,872
Your unburied crew.

1000
01:29:39,418 --> 01:29:41,628
Demanding you share their dishonor.

1001
01:29:43,630 --> 01:29:44,840
Let's go!

1002
01:30:00,480 --> 01:30:01,773
Cast off!

1003
01:30:02,566 --> 01:30:04,818
Cast off! Move it!

1004
01:30:29,759 --> 01:30:30,802
Is that our fate?

1005
01:30:33,680 --> 01:30:35,348
Is that our fate?

1006
01:30:55,994 --> 01:30:58,121
We rowed out of Hell,

1007
01:30:59,206 --> 01:31:01,666
leaving so many fallen comrades.

1008
01:31:02,459 --> 01:31:04,294
I have to save the rest.

1009
01:31:05,003 --> 01:31:06,046
What's happened to them?

1010
01:31:07,047 --> 01:31:08,340
Did they leave me here?

1011
01:31:08,423 --> 01:31:10,050
Are they coming back?

1012
01:31:11,968 --> 01:31:13,595
Did you do something to them?

1013
01:31:15,180 --> 01:31:16,890
Did you do something to them?

1014
01:31:16,973 --> 01:31:18,141
Have some.

1015
01:31:18,767 --> 01:31:20,977
You told me not to eat the lotus flower.

1016
01:31:21,061 --> 01:31:22,103
Just tonight.

1017
01:31:22,938 --> 01:31:24,606
So you can get some sleep.

1018
01:31:27,901 --> 01:31:31,488
And tomorrow, when you're ready,
you'll remember.

1019
01:31:34,199 --> 01:31:36,326
You'll remember your crew.

1020
01:31:36,409 --> 01:31:37,869
And your family.

1021
01:31:37,953 --> 01:31:39,162
Family.

1022
01:31:40,914 --> 01:31:42,040
A wife?

1023
01:31:43,875 --> 01:31:45,293
Do I have a wife?

1024
01:31:49,881 --> 01:31:52,759
- What are you doing?
- It's all right, Melantho.

1025
01:31:52,842 --> 01:31:54,469
Strangers don't come in here.

1026
01:31:54,553 --> 01:31:55,762
Screen or no screen,

1027
01:31:55,845 --> 01:31:59,224
after three years of feasting together,
we're far from strangers, Penelope.

1028
01:31:59,307 --> 01:32:02,561
Are you trying to marry me
or ruin my reputation?

1029
01:32:02,644 --> 01:32:03,687
Melantho can be discreet.

1030
01:32:04,396 --> 01:32:07,816
I'm actually here out of concern
for your reputation.

1031
01:32:08,984 --> 01:32:10,610
Everyone sees how hard you work.

1032
01:32:10,694 --> 01:32:13,238
Slow, steady progress.

1033
01:32:14,614 --> 01:32:16,992
But, every night, you've been
unraveling that work.

1034
01:32:22,789 --> 01:32:23,832
Leave us.

1035
01:32:23,915 --> 01:32:26,751
You can't expect her

1036
01:32:26,835 --> 01:32:29,296
to be loyal to a master
she never even knew.

1037
01:32:29,379 --> 01:32:31,256
I expect her to be loyal to me.

1038
01:32:31,339 --> 01:32:32,299
And she will be.

1039
01:32:32,382 --> 01:32:34,009
When you're married to me.

1040
01:32:35,051 --> 01:32:38,597
Slaves have no choice
but to be loyal to the future

1041
01:32:38,680 --> 01:32:40,890
and this is a household
waiting for a master,

1042
01:32:40,974 --> 01:32:43,560
just as Ithaca's a kingdom
waiting for a king.

1043
01:32:45,979 --> 01:32:47,772
Ithaca's king is coming back.

1044
01:32:47,856 --> 01:32:49,190
No, he's not.

1045
01:32:51,067 --> 01:32:53,820
Do you know that, as a boy,
I tried to go with him to Troy?

1046
01:32:55,071 --> 01:32:56,323
He never told me that.

1047
01:32:57,866 --> 01:32:59,117
Odysseus refused to let me.

1048
01:32:59,200 --> 01:33:02,662
He knew that Ithaca
needed a future without him.

1049
01:33:09,419 --> 01:33:11,504
And he saw me as part of that future.

1050
01:33:16,259 --> 01:33:21,389
I'll never tell other suitors
about your... Deception.

1051
01:33:23,725 --> 01:33:25,226
Finish your weaving.

1052
01:33:27,270 --> 01:33:28,688
It's time to let him go.

1053
01:33:37,989 --> 01:33:40,575
Why would you want to marry someone
who loves someone else?

1054
01:33:43,578 --> 01:33:48,249
Your loyalty to Odysseus
is one of your finest qualities.

1055
01:33:51,586 --> 01:33:52,921
I love you.

1056
01:33:53,546 --> 01:33:55,340
I want you to choose me.

1057
01:33:55,423 --> 01:33:57,759
And end all this.

1058
01:33:58,301 --> 01:34:00,887
You can't tell me you're not tired.

1059
01:34:01,596 --> 01:34:04,182
The waiting. The uncertainty.

1060
01:34:06,309 --> 01:34:08,895
You've done your duty.

1061
01:34:10,063 --> 01:34:13,692
Year after year after year.

1062
01:34:16,569 --> 01:34:18,154
It's time to think of yourself.

1063
01:34:19,989 --> 01:34:21,408
It's time to live again.

1064
01:34:26,913 --> 01:34:28,581
It's what Odysseus would've wanted.

1065
01:34:35,505 --> 01:34:36,881
I'll take care of all your needs.

1066
01:34:41,678 --> 01:34:42,804
My Queen.

1067
01:34:45,932 --> 01:34:47,475
And my son?

1068
01:34:48,601 --> 01:34:50,061
The throne is his.

1069
01:34:50,145 --> 01:34:51,146
He's the heir.

1070
01:34:53,648 --> 01:34:56,067
Unless we have a child of our own.

1071
01:35:06,244 --> 01:35:07,746
Where is Telemachus?

1072
01:35:09,330 --> 01:35:10,790
He's gone inland.

1073
01:35:10,874 --> 01:35:13,126
To visit his grandfather.

1074
01:35:20,508 --> 01:35:21,968
She's lying.

1075
01:35:22,051 --> 01:35:23,511
He's gone to sea.

1076
01:35:37,984 --> 01:35:39,694
He's gone for news of his father.

1077
01:35:40,320 --> 01:35:41,321
Where?

1078
01:35:41,404 --> 01:35:43,990
He's gone to Sparta. To Menelaus.

1079
01:35:45,325 --> 01:35:46,785
Well, should we go there?

1080
01:35:47,577 --> 01:35:48,745
No.

1081
01:35:49,913 --> 01:35:53,249
Too loud, Melanthius.
I told you you're a cowherd.

1082
01:35:54,375 --> 01:35:55,710
- He was listening.
- No, no.

1083
01:35:55,794 --> 01:35:57,086
No. No.

1084
01:35:59,464 --> 01:36:00,507
I was coming for Argus.

1085
01:36:02,133 --> 01:36:03,551
I told you that dog stays there.

1086
01:36:03,635 --> 01:36:05,011
Go back to the farm.

1087
01:36:05,094 --> 01:36:06,763
When we need your pigs,
we'll send Melanthius.

1088
01:36:07,764 --> 01:36:09,974
You're no longer welcome
in this palace, old man.

1089
01:36:10,058 --> 01:36:12,018
I answer to my mistress.

1090
01:36:14,354 --> 01:36:15,855
She even know your name?

1091
01:36:17,190 --> 01:36:18,233
Now go.

1092
01:36:18,817 --> 01:36:22,070
Telemachus will have to come back
by the temple at Pylos.

1093
01:36:22,946 --> 01:36:24,447
Take your men and wait for him there.

1094
01:36:24,531 --> 01:36:26,783
Why, so you can stay
and try and charm the Queen?

1095
01:36:26,866 --> 01:36:28,201
I don't think so.

1096
01:36:29,118 --> 01:36:30,787
He needs to die on that road.

1097
01:36:30,870 --> 01:36:32,497
And Irus has a plan.

1098
01:36:33,289 --> 01:36:34,457
Irus!

1099
01:36:36,793 --> 01:36:38,503
I need everyone in his party dead.

1100
01:36:38,586 --> 01:36:40,588
In a ditch. Throats open.

1101
01:36:40,672 --> 01:36:43,091
Telemachus and his men
will be ready for the bandits

1102
01:36:43,174 --> 01:36:45,426
who work the road between here and Pylos.

1103
01:36:46,469 --> 01:36:48,763
But if some of them took
the temple from the priests

1104
01:36:48,847 --> 01:36:49,889
before they arrived...

1105
01:36:51,307 --> 01:36:53,351
And bring me part of Telemachus as proof.

1106
01:36:54,853 --> 01:36:55,937
His nose.

1107
01:36:56,563 --> 01:36:58,356
Would you know him only by his nose?

1108
01:37:01,943 --> 01:37:03,194
His ears too.

1109
01:37:08,408 --> 01:37:10,034
Who taught you?

1110
01:37:10,118 --> 01:37:11,661
My father's swineherd.

1111
01:37:11,744 --> 01:37:12,829
The swineherder.

1112
01:37:12,912 --> 01:37:14,539
Yeah, his most loyal servant.

1113
01:37:15,123 --> 01:37:18,126
Odysseus left his most
loyal servant behind.

1114
01:37:18,209 --> 01:37:19,669
He always was the smartest.

1115
01:37:20,670 --> 01:37:23,047
Only one Agamemnon ever listened to.

1116
01:37:23,882 --> 01:37:25,383
Should have listened harder.

1117
01:37:25,466 --> 01:37:27,302
Can I ask
what happened to your brother?

1118
01:37:27,385 --> 01:37:28,636
His wife betrayed him.

1119
01:37:28,720 --> 01:37:29,888
Murdered him.

1120
01:37:30,555 --> 01:37:31,598
What did you do to them?

1121
01:37:31,681 --> 01:37:33,850
His son Orestes, he killed them both.

1122
01:37:34,726 --> 01:37:36,060
What, he killed his own mother?

1123
01:37:37,353 --> 01:37:38,396
He avenged his father.

1124
01:37:39,480 --> 01:37:40,857
But you don't have to worry.

1125
01:37:40,940 --> 01:37:43,651
Your mother has nothing
in common with Clytemnestra.

1126
01:37:43,735 --> 01:37:44,736
Maybe not.

1127
01:37:44,819 --> 01:37:46,654
But the suitors have convinced the elders

1128
01:37:46,738 --> 01:37:48,865
it's time she remarried.

1129
01:37:48,948 --> 01:37:50,950
It's not a good time for an empty throne.

1130
01:37:51,451 --> 01:37:53,578
You've heard the stories.
People from the sea.

1131
01:37:53,661 --> 01:37:54,829
Have you been raided?

1132
01:37:54,913 --> 01:37:57,332
Not yet, but the attacks,
they do seem closer.

1133
01:37:57,415 --> 01:37:58,791
Sparta's ready.

1134
01:37:58,875 --> 01:38:00,627
What about Ithaca?

1135
01:38:00,710 --> 01:38:02,962
Would she be able to raise an army,
defend herself?

1136
01:38:03,046 --> 01:38:04,422
Someone's gotta take the throne.

1137
01:38:04,505 --> 01:38:06,966
Well, then the battle is there,
not here, son.

1138
01:38:07,759 --> 01:38:09,344
You've run from the problem.

1139
01:38:10,261 --> 01:38:12,764
And left your mother
at the mercy of the suitors.

1140
01:38:14,098 --> 01:38:15,934
Should I hire mercenaries?

1141
01:38:16,017 --> 01:38:18,269
Go back and kill them all?

1142
01:38:18,353 --> 01:38:20,480
Murder rich and powerful
guests in your own home?

1143
01:38:21,147 --> 01:38:22,607
You'd be exiled.

1144
01:38:22,690 --> 01:38:24,275
What then?

1145
01:38:24,359 --> 01:38:27,820
You must evict them
without violating Zeus' Law.

1146
01:38:29,364 --> 01:38:30,949
I have to take the throne.

1147
01:38:32,241 --> 01:38:34,160
I have to know if Odysseus is alive.

1148
01:38:34,702 --> 01:38:37,372
This is nothing to do with
a father you've never known.

1149
01:38:37,997 --> 01:38:39,749
You have to lead, son.

1150
01:38:39,832 --> 01:38:41,376
No matter what's happened to Odysseus.

1151
01:38:55,640 --> 01:38:57,016
You need to remember.

1152
01:38:57,100 --> 01:38:58,393
No, I don't.

1153
01:38:59,310 --> 01:39:02,021
I don't need to know the truth.
I'm happy here. With you.

1154
01:39:04,315 --> 01:39:08,111
Then why are you gathering
the bones of your old ship to make a raft?

1155
01:39:10,822 --> 01:39:12,323
Remember your crew.

1156
01:39:13,741 --> 01:39:14,784
Your journey.

1157
01:39:18,830 --> 01:39:21,499
I heard the song of the sirens.

1158
01:39:30,883 --> 01:39:32,760
Put the wax in the men's ears.

1159
01:39:33,720 --> 01:39:36,472
- None for me. Tie me to the mast.
- Why?

1160
01:39:36,556 --> 01:39:38,016
I wanna hear the sirens' song.

1161
01:39:38,099 --> 01:39:39,017
No.

1162
01:39:39,976 --> 01:39:41,269
That's a bad idea.

1163
01:39:41,352 --> 01:39:42,770
No one's ever heard the song and lived.

1164
01:39:42,854 --> 01:39:45,148
That's why it's a good idea.
I'll be the first.

1165
01:39:46,232 --> 01:39:47,608
No matter how hard I plead,

1166
01:39:47,692 --> 01:39:50,111
do not untie me until we're well clear.

1167
01:39:51,362 --> 01:39:52,905
Check the men's ears.

1168
01:39:53,656 --> 01:39:56,451
A week's rations
for the first, "Yes, sir"!

1169
01:40:54,926 --> 01:40:56,427
What was the sirens' song?

1170
01:40:57,720 --> 01:40:59,847
All the things
you want it to be.

1171
01:41:00,598 --> 01:41:04,268
Then all the things
you wish you never wished for.

1172
01:41:22,203 --> 01:41:25,414
It was the delicious itch
you go to scratch,

1173
01:41:26,541 --> 01:41:29,043
but find it's under the skin.
You can't reach it.

1174
01:41:31,337 --> 01:41:34,090
So the delicious itch becomes unbearable.

1175
01:41:38,386 --> 01:41:40,513
Told you what you most want

1176
01:41:42,515 --> 01:41:44,517
is what you most can't have.

1177
01:41:48,104 --> 01:41:49,939
And what you most can't have

1178
01:41:51,232 --> 01:41:53,401
is what you already had.

1179
01:41:53,484 --> 01:41:55,069
And lost.

1180
01:42:03,327 --> 01:42:05,872
It was the song of all
the promises I failed to keep.

1181
01:42:11,210 --> 01:42:13,671
And it told me I don't really
wanna go home.

1182
01:42:16,716 --> 01:42:18,634
White water!

1183
01:42:18,718 --> 01:42:19,760
White water ahead!

1184
01:42:22,680 --> 01:42:23,723
Charybdis.

1185
01:42:24,765 --> 01:42:26,434
A monstrous whirlpool.

1186
01:42:29,145 --> 01:42:30,146
Hoist the sails!

1187
01:42:30,813 --> 01:42:32,231
Come on, let's go!

1188
01:42:32,315 --> 01:42:34,192
Get the windlass up!

1189
01:42:34,275 --> 01:42:35,610
Can we head out to sea?

1190
01:42:35,693 --> 01:42:36,986
It's already got us.

1191
01:42:38,988 --> 01:42:40,406
Man those ropes!

1192
01:42:40,489 --> 01:42:42,283
We wanna head north! Move it!

1193
01:42:43,576 --> 01:42:45,620
- We might make that canyon.
- It's too narrow.

1194
01:42:45,703 --> 01:42:47,496
Make for the whirlpool.

1195
01:42:47,580 --> 01:42:49,123
If you steer down the slope,

1196
01:42:49,207 --> 01:42:51,626
we can make enough way
to shoot out the other side.

1197
01:42:51,709 --> 01:42:53,377
Don't be afraid. We can do it.

1198
01:42:58,382 --> 01:43:00,218
No. No!

1199
01:43:00,927 --> 01:43:02,345
We're making for the channel.

1200
01:43:02,428 --> 01:43:04,180
- Get a grip.
- We're making for the channel!

1201
01:43:04,263 --> 01:43:05,306
Oars in!

1202
01:43:07,350 --> 01:43:08,392
Get the oars in!

1203
01:43:09,143 --> 01:43:11,812
Starboard oars, prepare to hold water!

1204
01:43:18,527 --> 01:43:19,737
On my signal!

1205
01:43:21,155 --> 01:43:22,156
Three...

1206
01:43:24,367 --> 01:43:25,493
Two...

1207
01:43:26,869 --> 01:43:28,371
One! Hard!

1208
01:43:29,705 --> 01:43:32,041
Oars out!

1209
01:43:42,134 --> 01:43:43,177
Hold it!

1210
01:44:16,752 --> 01:44:18,254
We made it!

1211
01:44:18,337 --> 01:44:19,338
We did!

1212
01:44:20,256 --> 01:44:22,133
We did it. The gods were with us...

1213
01:44:25,845 --> 01:44:26,804
Pull!

1214
01:44:28,222 --> 01:44:29,265
Pull!

1215
01:44:29,974 --> 01:44:31,517
Pull!

1216
01:44:31,600 --> 01:44:32,810
Pull!

1217
01:44:32,893 --> 01:44:34,562
Keep us straight!

1218
01:44:36,981 --> 01:44:38,399
Pull!

1219
01:44:40,526 --> 01:44:41,819
Pull!

1220
01:44:41,902 --> 01:44:43,195
Keep us straight!

1221
01:44:43,279 --> 01:44:46,741
Pull! Pull!

1222
01:44:49,118 --> 01:44:50,119
Pull!

1223
01:45:10,264 --> 01:45:11,265
You knew.

1224
01:45:11,807 --> 01:45:12,850
And you didn't tell us.

1225
01:45:13,726 --> 01:45:15,853
If I told you,
you wouldn't have chosen this way.

1226
01:45:15,936 --> 01:45:17,938
And those men would still be alive!

1227
01:45:18,022 --> 01:45:19,023
No!

1228
01:45:19,106 --> 01:45:20,566
We'd all be dead.

1229
01:45:20,649 --> 01:45:22,193
You had no right!

1230
01:45:22,276 --> 01:45:23,611
I had every right.

1231
01:45:23,694 --> 01:45:25,321
And every responsibility!

1232
01:45:25,404 --> 01:45:28,449
The whirlpool would have
destroyed the ship and all of us.

1233
01:45:28,532 --> 01:45:29,575
This way, we live.

1234
01:45:29,658 --> 01:45:30,743
Some of us live!

1235
01:45:30,826 --> 01:45:32,244
Yes!

1236
01:45:35,539 --> 01:45:36,999
What else did Tiresias tell you?

1237
01:45:49,095 --> 01:45:50,513
I'm gonna put in there.

1238
01:45:52,932 --> 01:45:56,227
We can't land there.
That's the Sun God's isle.

1239
01:45:56,310 --> 01:45:57,686
We need repairs.

1240
01:45:58,896 --> 01:46:00,648
These men are exhausted!

1241
01:46:00,731 --> 01:46:01,982
We lost six shipmates!

1242
01:46:02,608 --> 01:46:05,194
We're gonna put in.
We're gonna rest up on dry land.

1243
01:46:05,277 --> 01:46:07,279
You're all gonna die!

1244
01:46:09,865 --> 01:46:11,867
You asked me what Tiresias told me?

1245
01:46:12,785 --> 01:46:14,328
He said you're all gonna die.

1246
01:46:14,995 --> 01:46:20,126
If you slaughter the Sun God's cattle
which graze on that island.

1247
01:46:20,209 --> 01:46:21,293
You'll all die.

1248
01:46:25,631 --> 01:46:26,674
That's fine.

1249
01:46:27,633 --> 01:46:28,759
We'll put in.

1250
01:46:29,468 --> 01:46:30,719
We'll cook our own provisions.

1251
01:46:30,803 --> 01:46:33,889
We'll pick fruit. We'll get water.
Nothing more.

1252
01:46:35,182 --> 01:46:36,600
Swear to it.

1253
01:46:37,101 --> 01:46:38,269
All of you.

1254
01:46:52,074 --> 01:46:53,075
See?

1255
01:46:54,076 --> 01:46:55,619
The wind took us right in.

1256
01:46:56,745 --> 01:46:59,707
We'll rest up. Do the repairs.

1257
01:46:59,790 --> 01:47:02,042
We'll set out as soon as the wind changes.

1258
01:47:02,793 --> 01:47:03,794
Grab that line.

1259
01:47:14,972 --> 01:47:16,974
Did the crew keep their promise?

1260
01:47:18,017 --> 01:47:19,143
Yes.

1261
01:47:19,768 --> 01:47:21,145
For a long time.

1262
01:47:22,730 --> 01:47:24,273
But the wind never changed.

1263
01:47:24,356 --> 01:47:28,402
The steady, on-shore breeze
that made it so easy for us to land

1264
01:47:30,905 --> 01:47:32,448
made it impossible to leave.

1265
01:47:36,452 --> 01:47:38,412
And our provisions ran out.

1266
01:47:41,624 --> 01:47:42,708
Go.

1267
01:48:45,312 --> 01:48:48,524
Did you think if you ate quietly
the Sun God wouldn't notice?

1268
01:48:48,607 --> 01:48:50,317
We thought you wouldn't notice.

1269
01:48:50,943 --> 01:48:52,444
I'm not the problem.

1270
01:48:52,528 --> 01:48:54,154
I wanted you to live.

1271
01:48:59,827 --> 01:49:01,287
Tiresias told you.

1272
01:49:01,370 --> 01:49:03,622
Circe told you. The gods told you.

1273
01:49:04,290 --> 01:49:06,750
You gods don't speak
in ways we understand.

1274
01:49:06,834 --> 01:49:08,502
Who doesn't understand pain?

1275
01:49:09,336 --> 01:49:10,754
Or blood or death?

1276
01:49:11,589 --> 01:49:14,675
When you watch your men die, do you
see chance or do you see consequence?

1277
01:49:15,217 --> 01:49:17,886
I see consequence. I see my failure.

1278
01:49:18,387 --> 01:49:21,098
What if their deaths aren't your failure
but theirs?

1279
01:49:21,181 --> 01:49:22,433
I lead them.

1280
01:49:23,851 --> 01:49:25,394
I led them here.

1281
01:49:25,477 --> 01:49:26,854
And who led you?

1282
01:49:27,980 --> 01:49:29,565
Why do you put yourself above the gods?

1283
01:49:29,648 --> 01:49:31,483
Why do you claim responsibility?

1284
01:49:32,443 --> 01:49:34,111
Why don't you wanna go home?

1285
01:49:34,194 --> 01:49:35,863
What do you expect?

1286
01:49:37,323 --> 01:49:38,824
They ran out of food.

1287
01:49:39,867 --> 01:49:40,868
Have some pity.

1288
01:49:40,951 --> 01:49:43,871
My pity is what put us
on this damned island.

1289
01:49:43,954 --> 01:49:45,289
I told you what would happen.

1290
01:49:45,372 --> 01:49:46,498
Yes, you did.

1291
01:49:47,207 --> 01:49:50,294
And we decided we would
rather drown than starve.

1292
01:49:55,799 --> 01:49:58,010
Well, now's our chance.

1293
01:49:58,093 --> 01:50:00,346
The wind has dropped!
The wind has dropped!

1294
01:50:04,308 --> 01:50:08,103
The calm was just long enough
to lure us out onto Poseidon's ocean.

1295
01:50:09,396 --> 01:50:11,231
Under Zeus' sky.

1296
01:50:11,315 --> 01:50:14,443
Get us into the wind!

1297
01:50:14,526 --> 01:50:16,487
Port! Port!

1298
01:50:30,334 --> 01:50:31,835
Pray to the gods!

1299
01:50:32,961 --> 01:50:35,881
- Get us into the wind!
- Pray for forgiveness!

1300
01:50:35,964 --> 01:50:37,216
Wind!

1301
01:50:52,856 --> 01:50:54,692
Get us into the wind!

1302
01:50:55,484 --> 01:50:56,985
Into the wind!

1303
01:50:59,613 --> 01:51:01,156
Odysseus!

1304
01:51:02,783 --> 01:51:04,284
Get us into the wind!

1305
01:51:17,798 --> 01:51:19,675
Give yourself to the gods!

1306
01:51:23,679 --> 01:51:25,973
Pray to Poseidon!

1307
01:51:32,438 --> 01:51:34,982
Pray for forgiveness!

1308
01:51:46,827 --> 01:51:47,995
Look out!

1309
01:52:03,010 --> 01:52:05,929
The last thing I remember
was the harsh hand of Zeus.

1310
01:52:18,358 --> 01:52:20,068
Eurylochus!

1311
01:52:50,891 --> 01:52:52,309
I don't know what happened to my men.

1312
01:52:52,392 --> 01:52:53,602
Yes, you do.

1313
01:52:55,687 --> 01:52:57,481
Exactly what you told them would happen.

1314
01:52:57,981 --> 01:52:59,942
I washed up on your shore alone.

1315
01:53:00,025 --> 01:53:02,402
You washed up on my shore

1316
01:53:02,486 --> 01:53:04,988
with some of your boat
and none of your crew.

1317
01:53:06,990 --> 01:53:09,243
Half dead, unconscious.

1318
01:53:31,515 --> 01:53:34,017
I watched you lost for days...

1319
01:53:34,101 --> 01:53:35,894
No, no, no.

1320
01:53:35,978 --> 01:53:37,646
...into weeks.

1321
01:53:38,313 --> 01:53:40,190
I knew the lotus
would ease your awakening.

1322
01:53:40,274 --> 01:53:42,568
No, no, no.

1323
01:53:42,651 --> 01:53:45,362
So that your pain
was more of the body than the mind.

1324
01:53:48,115 --> 01:53:49,241
And it did.

1325
01:53:51,785 --> 01:53:53,537
You liked it so much.

1326
01:53:54,705 --> 01:53:56,665
You ate it so willingly.

1327
01:54:00,377 --> 01:54:01,795
And as I cared for you...

1328
01:54:02,421 --> 01:54:03,547
Thank you.

1329
01:54:03,630 --> 01:54:05,549
...I came to love you.

1330
01:54:10,596 --> 01:54:12,306
How long have I been here?

1331
01:54:14,391 --> 01:54:17,060
I kept telling myself
to stop giving it to you,

1332
01:54:17,144 --> 01:54:19,104
that you were strong enough to remember.

1333
01:54:19,187 --> 01:54:21,315
- How long, Calypso?
- I mean, you were so happy.

1334
01:54:21,398 --> 01:54:24,943
- You were happy.
- We were so happy. For years.

1335
01:54:25,027 --> 01:54:26,111
Years?

1336
01:54:33,035 --> 01:54:34,036
Seven years.

1337
01:54:35,287 --> 01:54:38,874
You fed me lotus flower
and held me captive.

1338
01:54:38,957 --> 01:54:40,167
I saved your life.

1339
01:54:41,335 --> 01:54:42,377
I rebuilt your body.

1340
01:54:42,461 --> 01:54:45,380
I just couldn't bring myself
to rebuild your mind.

1341
01:54:46,548 --> 01:54:48,091
You have been happy.

1342
01:54:49,176 --> 01:54:52,387
Your heart was just always somewhere else.

1343
01:54:53,513 --> 01:54:57,142
And your mind was bound
to follow eventually.

1344
01:54:57,976 --> 01:54:59,311
How could you?

1345
01:54:59,394 --> 01:55:02,564
The lotus clouds the mind
and saps the will,

1346
01:55:02,648 --> 01:55:04,650
but it doesn't change who you are.

1347
01:55:05,233 --> 01:55:08,070
You weren't ready to go home,
for whatever reason.

1348
01:55:08,153 --> 01:55:09,821
I am now.

1349
01:55:11,323 --> 01:55:13,075
As I always knew you would be.

1350
01:55:13,700 --> 01:55:14,701
How?

1351
01:55:14,785 --> 01:55:17,704
You were clutching something
when I found you.

1352
01:55:17,788 --> 01:55:19,456
Your hand wouldn't open for weeks.

1353
01:55:20,415 --> 01:55:21,792
You'd call out.

1354
01:55:21,875 --> 01:55:23,543
Mostly to Athena,

1355
01:55:23,627 --> 01:55:26,546
but sometimes you would say another name.

1356
01:55:40,644 --> 01:55:42,062
Penelope.

1357
01:55:46,024 --> 01:55:47,526
Help me go home.

1358
01:55:48,151 --> 01:55:49,319
I am.

1359
01:55:50,570 --> 01:55:53,115
You're a man
who needs to control his fate.

1360
01:55:54,616 --> 01:55:56,159
But you cannot control this.

1361
01:55:59,454 --> 01:56:01,665
You must give yourself to the storm.

1362
01:56:02,332 --> 01:56:03,959
Give yourself to Poseidon.

1363
01:56:05,419 --> 01:56:06,920
And take your punishment.

1364
01:56:09,715 --> 01:56:11,299
Zeus won't let him kill you.

1365
01:56:14,928 --> 01:56:18,390
Give up the fight,
give up control, and live.

1366
01:56:21,226 --> 01:56:25,105
It's a leap of faith
you've never yet made, Odysseus.

1367
01:57:17,115 --> 01:57:18,116
Odysseus.

1368
01:57:26,374 --> 01:57:27,417
Where am I?

1369
01:57:29,544 --> 01:57:31,338
Don't you recognize your own home?

1370
01:58:10,752 --> 01:58:12,796
Zeus welcomes you, strangers.

1371
01:58:13,588 --> 01:58:14,589
How many are you?

1372
01:58:15,173 --> 01:58:16,174
Half a dozen.

1373
01:58:16,883 --> 01:58:17,968
In need of a meal?

1374
01:58:18,051 --> 01:58:19,344
And supplies.

1375
01:58:20,053 --> 01:58:21,221
For the road.

1376
01:58:21,304 --> 01:58:22,556
Where you headed?

1377
01:58:22,639 --> 01:58:24,057
We need pigs.

1378
01:58:24,141 --> 01:58:25,851
We're going all the way to Pylos.

1379
01:58:25,934 --> 01:58:27,811
Are you pilgrims?

1380
01:58:27,894 --> 01:58:29,855
Do we look like pilgrims?

1381
01:58:29,938 --> 01:58:31,314
I don't see well.

1382
01:58:32,524 --> 01:58:36,027
As Zeus demands, I offer you a meal
but these pigs aren't mine to sell.

1383
01:58:36,111 --> 01:58:37,487
Oh, don't worry.

1384
01:58:37,988 --> 01:58:38,822
We're not paying.

1385
01:58:42,492 --> 01:58:44,870
Give my greetings to your mother.

1386
01:58:47,914 --> 01:58:49,457
Tell her I'm sorry.

1387
01:58:49,541 --> 01:58:51,001
I'm sorry for Troy

1388
01:58:51,084 --> 01:58:53,295
and for everything
that has happened in my name.

1389
01:58:59,885 --> 01:59:02,345
I'm sorry I had no news of your father.

1390
01:59:02,429 --> 01:59:03,471
No, you had news.

1391
01:59:03,555 --> 01:59:06,099
Not of where he is, but who he is.

1392
01:59:07,142 --> 01:59:09,686
And now, more than ever,
I hope to one day meet him.

1393
01:59:14,816 --> 01:59:16,902
Where are your watchdogs, old man?

1394
01:59:21,364 --> 01:59:23,491
I'm burning their bodies.

1395
01:59:27,787 --> 01:59:30,624
Can Zeus offer you
food and drink, stranger?

1396
01:59:31,541 --> 01:59:33,168
Were you attacked?

1397
01:59:33,251 --> 01:59:35,045
The dogs saved your farmhands.

1398
01:59:35,128 --> 01:59:37,631
No, we burned his brothers first.

1399
01:59:40,425 --> 01:59:42,344
What has happened to Ithaca?

1400
01:59:42,427 --> 01:59:43,428
Darkness.

1401
01:59:43,511 --> 01:59:46,389
Zeus' Law smashed to pieces.

1402
01:59:47,182 --> 01:59:50,352
A kingdom without a king
since my master died.

1403
01:59:50,435 --> 01:59:51,436
Your master.

1404
01:59:52,479 --> 01:59:53,897
Odysseus.

1405
01:59:53,980 --> 01:59:56,024
Hero of the Trojan War.

1406
01:59:58,401 --> 02:00:00,028
But Odysseus is alive.

1407
02:00:00,111 --> 02:00:02,030
Beggars giving false hope!

1408
02:00:02,113 --> 02:00:04,199
No more! No more!

1409
02:00:04,282 --> 02:00:06,743
No more! No more. No more.

1410
02:00:06,826 --> 02:00:08,245
No more. No more.

1411
02:00:08,328 --> 02:00:11,414
Telemachus. Telemachus.

1412
02:00:11,498 --> 02:00:12,499
Telemachus.

1413
02:00:12,582 --> 02:00:15,835
He's in danger,
and I can't help him.

1414
02:00:15,919 --> 02:00:19,547
There must be others in Ithaca
that would help Odysseus' son.

1415
02:00:19,631 --> 02:00:22,092
The palace is controlled by suitors.

1416
02:00:22,676 --> 02:00:25,178
No one's still loyal to Odysseus.

1417
02:00:26,388 --> 02:00:27,722
What about the Queen?

1418
02:00:28,348 --> 02:00:30,725
Beautiful, wise Penelope.

1419
02:00:31,476 --> 02:00:33,812
Held hostage by the worst men.

1420
02:00:33,895 --> 02:00:36,147
Soon to be forced into marriage.

1421
02:00:36,231 --> 02:00:38,608
What could she do without even her son?

1422
02:00:38,692 --> 02:00:40,026
Here. Drink.

1423
02:00:44,155 --> 02:00:46,366
What's the danger to Telemachus?

1424
02:00:47,409 --> 02:00:49,577
He's on his way back from Sparta.

1425
02:00:50,787 --> 02:00:55,166
The suitors sent bandits to Pylos
to ambush him at the temple.

1426
02:00:56,293 --> 02:00:57,752
Give me food for the road.

1427
02:00:58,253 --> 02:01:01,089
You'll... You'll never
catch up with the bandits.

1428
02:01:01,172 --> 02:01:04,968
If I can get to Pylos before
Telemachus arrives, I can warn him.

1429
02:01:05,051 --> 02:01:06,428
Why are you helping?

1430
02:01:07,387 --> 02:01:09,097
If I tell you, don't be angry.

1431
02:01:09,889 --> 02:01:11,599
I served with Odysseus. At Troy.

1432
02:01:11,683 --> 02:01:12,684
Really?

1433
02:01:14,144 --> 02:01:15,645
He was a harsh taskmaster.

1434
02:01:15,729 --> 02:01:16,813
Ah, but fair.

1435
02:01:18,273 --> 02:01:20,692
Fair and worthy of following.

1436
02:01:21,609 --> 02:01:22,610
You loved him?

1437
02:01:23,695 --> 02:01:25,030
Like a son.

1438
02:01:25,697 --> 02:01:28,074
Well, he's not dead.

1439
02:01:28,575 --> 02:01:30,785
Is... Is he coming home?

1440
02:01:30,869 --> 02:01:32,078
Soon.

1441
02:01:32,662 --> 02:01:34,831
Bringing vengeance.

1442
02:01:36,583 --> 02:01:38,043
Bringing it all.

1443
02:01:47,302 --> 02:01:50,096
I'll talk. We don't want
people realizing who you are.

1444
02:01:50,180 --> 02:01:52,098
Well, maybe we shouldn't
have brought a guard.

1445
02:01:52,849 --> 02:01:54,851
We'll be glad we did
if we have trouble on the road.

1446
02:02:06,529 --> 02:02:07,614
Get back! Move!

1447
02:02:07,697 --> 02:02:09,699
I have a message. Wait!

1448
02:02:11,493 --> 02:02:12,535
Eumaeus sent me!

1449
02:02:14,746 --> 02:02:15,747
Find that beggar.

1450
02:02:32,180 --> 02:02:34,682
Your men must wait out here.
The weapons too.

1451
02:02:51,533 --> 02:02:53,868
Zeus welcomes you, strangers.

1452
02:02:53,952 --> 02:02:55,703
Have you traveled far?

1453
02:02:55,787 --> 02:02:56,955
Not that far.

1454
02:02:58,164 --> 02:03:00,083
You're here to rest for the night.

1455
02:03:02,168 --> 02:03:04,421
We're here to make sacrifice to Athena.

1456
02:03:06,589 --> 02:03:09,551
And whatever hospitality you can offer
might be considered.

1457
02:03:09,634 --> 02:03:11,845
Of course.
Get that beggar out of here.

1458
02:03:11,928 --> 02:03:13,638
He has a message for me.

1459
02:03:14,639 --> 02:03:15,849
Eumaeus sent you?

1460
02:03:17,016 --> 02:03:18,101
Yes, sir.

1461
02:03:23,982 --> 02:03:25,442
Well, what's the message?

1462
02:03:26,651 --> 02:03:28,111
It's private.

1463
02:03:28,194 --> 02:03:29,779
These men are...

1464
02:03:30,738 --> 02:03:31,739
These men are priests.

1465
02:03:49,466 --> 02:03:51,843
This is your moment to leave, old man.

1466
02:03:51,926 --> 02:03:53,303
Go, stranger.

1467
02:03:56,181 --> 02:03:58,766
I already spared one old man this week.

1468
02:03:58,850 --> 02:04:00,018
But he was blind.

1469
02:04:00,101 --> 02:04:01,102
I won't spare...

1470
02:04:40,558 --> 02:04:42,477
Not bad for a beggar.

1471
02:04:42,560 --> 02:04:44,479
No one's born a beggar.

1472
02:04:44,562 --> 02:04:47,690
We'll take these priest robes,
put 'em over your armor.

1473
02:04:49,859 --> 02:04:52,445
- Help me with his body.
- We have to leave him.

1474
02:04:52,529 --> 02:04:55,073
- No.
- We have to slip out.

1475
02:04:55,156 --> 02:04:57,867
- That's not how you honor the dead.
- That is how you stay alive.

1476
02:04:57,951 --> 02:04:59,536
He was my teacher.

1477
02:05:00,620 --> 02:05:01,788
He might have been Athena in disguise.

1478
02:05:01,871 --> 02:05:03,373
His name was Mentor.

1479
02:05:04,123 --> 02:05:05,166
And when you are safe,

1480
02:05:05,250 --> 02:05:08,461
we'll travel to the unknown west
and we will honor him.

1481
02:05:09,128 --> 02:05:11,089
These men were sent by the suitors.

1482
02:05:11,172 --> 02:05:13,508
There will be more of them.
We need to get on the road.

1483
02:05:13,591 --> 02:05:15,593
- By night?
- Safer than staying here.

1484
02:05:15,677 --> 02:05:18,429
Mentor's at the heart
of Athena's oldest temple.

1485
02:05:19,055 --> 02:05:21,182
For now, we'll honor him in flame.

1486
02:05:30,942 --> 02:05:32,485
Fire!

1487
02:05:36,531 --> 02:05:38,449
Fire! Fire! Fire!

1488
02:05:38,533 --> 02:05:39,951
Fire! Fire!

1489
02:06:00,138 --> 02:06:02,056
So how does
a beggar come to know Mentor?

1490
02:06:02,140 --> 02:06:04,726
I told you.
I wasn't always a beggar.

1491
02:06:04,809 --> 02:06:06,644
You have wise eyes.

1492
02:06:07,312 --> 02:06:09,814
- Athena's eyes.
- Don't look for gods in men.

1493
02:06:09,897 --> 02:06:11,649
You'll just be disappointed.

1494
02:06:11,733 --> 02:06:13,359
Well, then tell me who you are.

1495
02:06:14,944 --> 02:06:16,321
My name is Sinon.

1496
02:06:16,946 --> 02:06:20,158
I'm an old soldier who fought
under your father at Troy.

1497
02:06:21,159 --> 02:06:22,702
You knew my father?

1498
02:06:22,785 --> 02:06:25,371
I've been taking orders from him
for longer than I care to remember.

1499
02:06:26,873 --> 02:06:28,166
Wait, he's alive?

1500
02:06:29,167 --> 02:06:30,209
He's alive.

1501
02:06:31,544 --> 02:06:32,837
Well, where is he?

1502
02:06:33,338 --> 02:06:34,547
He's close by.

1503
02:06:35,298 --> 02:06:37,550
He sent me ahead to assess the situation.

1504
02:06:39,052 --> 02:06:40,762
Well, Mother's
not gonna believe it.

1505
02:06:40,845 --> 02:06:42,221
No, you can't tell her.

1506
02:06:43,389 --> 02:06:44,682
What, nothing?

1507
02:06:45,642 --> 02:06:46,851
Tell her...

1508
02:06:47,852 --> 02:06:50,271
Tell her you decided
it's time she remarried.

1509
02:06:52,106 --> 02:06:53,358
He doesn't need to test my mother.

1510
02:06:53,441 --> 02:06:54,567
How could he doubt her?

1511
02:06:54,651 --> 02:06:56,110
He doesn't.

1512
02:06:56,194 --> 02:06:58,571
But she has every reason
to have forgotten him.

1513
02:07:00,406 --> 02:07:01,991
Are there any loyal servants?

1514
02:07:03,034 --> 02:07:04,118
Some.

1515
02:07:04,994 --> 02:07:06,454
When Odysseus shows himself, all.

1516
02:07:08,498 --> 02:07:10,667
We need to gather
the suitors at the palace.

1517
02:07:11,459 --> 02:07:13,294
That's never a problem.

1518
02:07:13,378 --> 02:07:15,338
You have gifts from Menelaus?

1519
02:07:15,421 --> 02:07:17,715
- Yeah, on my ship.
- Good.

1520
02:07:18,508 --> 02:07:19,634
Come back as a prince.

1521
02:07:20,134 --> 02:07:21,344
Call a banquet.

1522
02:07:21,844 --> 02:07:22,970
I'll be there.

1523
02:07:23,971 --> 02:07:25,431
What will you do at the banquet?

1524
02:07:26,057 --> 02:07:27,517
Beg.

1525
02:07:27,600 --> 02:07:29,811
The clearest view of a man is from below.

1526
02:07:31,688 --> 02:07:33,106
How many suitors are there?

1527
02:07:33,648 --> 02:07:34,899
Dozens.

1528
02:07:35,566 --> 02:07:37,026
But if all of his men are like you, then...

1529
02:07:37,110 --> 02:07:38,569
He doesn't have any men.

1530
02:07:39,445 --> 02:07:40,697
What, it's just you?

1531
02:07:41,906 --> 02:07:42,949
And you.

1532
02:07:48,955 --> 02:07:50,164
Telemachus is back.

1533
02:07:50,248 --> 02:07:51,958
- What?
- Telemachus is back.

1534
02:07:52,709 --> 02:07:55,169
- Who's saying this?
- Everyone's saying it.

1535
02:07:55,253 --> 02:07:56,379
Where's Telemachus?

1536
02:07:57,296 --> 02:07:59,882
My Queen! Surely your place is up there?

1537
02:08:08,057 --> 02:08:09,267
It's true?

1538
02:08:12,103 --> 02:08:13,104
You're home.

1539
02:08:14,063 --> 02:08:15,148
I'm back.

1540
02:08:15,732 --> 02:08:17,191
I thought tonight we could celebrate.

1541
02:08:18,359 --> 02:08:21,070
If we have anything left
to celebrate with.

1542
02:08:22,613 --> 02:08:24,073
Don't worry.

1543
02:08:24,157 --> 02:08:26,743
My brave suitors will shower us with gifts
to honor your return.

1544
02:08:27,326 --> 02:08:28,995
So glad to see your safe return.

1545
02:08:39,881 --> 02:08:41,424
Glad to see you on your feet.

1546
02:08:42,133 --> 02:08:44,010
It's about time we took you
back to the palace.

1547
02:08:47,680 --> 02:08:49,390
What did you hear about your father?

1548
02:08:51,058 --> 02:08:52,059
Nothing.

1549
02:08:52,894 --> 02:08:53,978
Stories from the war,

1550
02:08:54,061 --> 02:08:56,314
but no one knew what happened
to him after he left Troy.

1551
02:08:57,064 --> 02:08:58,191
I'm sorry, Mom.

1552
02:08:59,108 --> 02:09:02,904
Everyone in Sparta is talking
about a coming catastrophe.

1553
02:09:03,654 --> 02:09:07,492
An age of darkness brought on by people
from the sea violating Zeus' Law.

1554
02:09:07,575 --> 02:09:08,951
So the stories are true.

1555
02:09:09,035 --> 02:09:10,995
Our civilization is collapsing.

1556
02:09:11,496 --> 02:09:13,122
Menelaus is concerned
that we can't raise an army

1557
02:09:13,206 --> 02:09:14,540
with an empty throne.

1558
02:09:14,624 --> 02:09:16,083
So we came up with a plan.

1559
02:09:17,710 --> 02:09:18,711
What plan?

1560
02:09:20,880 --> 02:09:22,965
The one way to get the suitors to leave.

1561
02:09:25,259 --> 02:09:26,761
It's time for you to choose.

1562
02:09:30,640 --> 02:09:32,225
You want me to remarry?

1563
02:09:32,308 --> 02:09:35,061
If you remarry,
the other suitors will leave.

1564
02:09:35,144 --> 02:09:37,063
And then I'm only dealing
with one of them.

1565
02:09:37,146 --> 02:09:38,147
I'm almost of age.

1566
02:09:38,231 --> 02:09:41,275
I'll tell the elders that I learned
of Odysseus' death in Sparta.

1567
02:09:42,193 --> 02:09:45,196
Menelaus will back me up,
and they'll let me assume the throne.

1568
02:09:45,279 --> 02:09:47,406
So you deny your father,
and I leave my home

1569
02:09:47,490 --> 02:09:48,825
and go live with one of these snakes.

1570
02:09:48,908 --> 02:09:49,909
That's your plan.

1571
02:09:49,992 --> 02:09:51,536
Well, do you have a better one?

1572
02:09:54,205 --> 02:09:57,583
Lock the door, set a fire,
and burn the bastards.

1573
02:09:58,668 --> 02:10:02,380
Do you think if I remarry
life goes back to the way it was?

1574
02:10:03,339 --> 02:10:05,299
That world is gone.

1575
02:10:05,383 --> 02:10:06,425
And, in this one,

1576
02:10:06,509 --> 02:10:08,469
they're not giving up power to you.

1577
02:10:08,553 --> 02:10:09,554
Or Odysseus.

1578
02:10:09,637 --> 02:10:11,848
If he returned, he'd have to kill them.

1579
02:10:12,348 --> 02:10:13,516
Like you will.

1580
02:10:14,225 --> 02:10:17,144
If I kill them, their families
will demand revenge.

1581
02:10:17,228 --> 02:10:18,604
I'd be exiled.

1582
02:10:19,522 --> 02:10:21,148
And, without me, the throne'll be lost.

1583
02:10:21,232 --> 02:10:22,984
'Cause only a man can sit on it.

1584
02:10:24,777 --> 02:10:25,820
Empty throne?

1585
02:10:27,738 --> 02:10:31,242
I've been sitting on
that empty throne for 20 years!

1586
02:10:31,325 --> 02:10:33,953
My knowledge, my years of experience

1587
02:10:34,036 --> 02:10:36,789
that's all nothing compared
to the bristles on your chin.

1588
02:10:39,625 --> 02:10:40,918
Do you want peace?

1589
02:10:42,044 --> 02:10:43,754
- Or do you want revenge?
- I want Odysseus!

1590
02:10:47,341 --> 02:10:48,426
Sing...

1591
02:10:49,093 --> 02:10:50,469
Muse, of a face...

1592
02:10:53,890 --> 02:10:55,099
...a face...

1593
02:10:59,437 --> 02:11:00,521
...a fleet...

1594
02:11:04,859 --> 02:11:05,902
...a war...

1595
02:11:10,239 --> 02:11:11,407
...at Troy...

1596
02:11:15,453 --> 02:11:17,413
Sing, Muse, of a man...

1597
02:11:21,709 --> 02:11:22,752
...a man...

1598
02:11:27,006 --> 02:11:28,090
...a thought...

1599
02:11:32,094 --> 02:11:33,304
...a trick...

1600
02:11:37,016 --> 02:11:38,142
Eumaeus, go ahead.

1601
02:11:45,066 --> 02:11:46,317
Argus.

1602
02:12:09,215 --> 02:12:11,258
You should bury him up on the cliff.

1603
02:12:13,219 --> 02:12:14,428
Where you met?

1604
02:12:23,145 --> 02:12:24,480
You're Odysseus.

1605
02:12:27,566 --> 02:12:29,110
You're my father.

1606
02:12:31,654 --> 02:12:32,738
I'm a beggar.

1607
02:12:36,242 --> 02:12:37,368
And you

1608
02:12:39,412 --> 02:12:40,788
are the worthy,

1609
02:12:42,123 --> 02:12:45,084
beautiful son of Odysseus.

1610
02:12:46,752 --> 02:12:50,006
Of whom, when you do finally meet,

1611
02:12:52,091 --> 02:12:53,634
he will be so proud.

1612
02:12:57,596 --> 02:13:00,224
Welcome home, stranger.

1613
02:13:03,686 --> 02:13:05,271
Because the darkness of victory

1614
02:13:06,564 --> 02:13:08,649
shrouds the writers of legends...

1615
02:13:10,776 --> 02:13:13,320
...and make bards
and blind beggars of us all.

1616
02:13:20,828 --> 02:13:22,747
Accept
the hospitality of Zeus.

1617
02:13:23,414 --> 02:13:25,249
Get the stranger some food.

1618
02:13:25,332 --> 02:13:26,333
No, sir.

1619
02:13:27,168 --> 02:13:29,211
I'll just make the rounds with a bowl.

1620
02:13:34,633 --> 02:13:37,261
Had to invite another one
of these filthy plate-lickers to dinner?

1621
02:13:37,344 --> 02:13:39,472
What's one more beggar at this feast?

1622
02:13:57,364 --> 02:13:59,867
We need to kill him before he regains
his position with the elders.

1623
02:13:59,950 --> 02:14:01,160
How?

1624
02:14:16,509 --> 02:14:17,510
Bring the wine! Bring it!

1625
02:14:17,593 --> 02:14:19,053
Provoke him.

1626
02:14:19,637 --> 02:14:21,472
Get him to grab one of the weapons.

1627
02:14:25,726 --> 02:14:28,521
He's not the same young pup
we used to bait.

1628
02:14:28,604 --> 02:14:30,940
Then we come back tonight,
and we cut his throat.

1629
02:14:32,566 --> 02:14:34,193
- Fuck off, old man.
- Please.

1630
02:14:35,027 --> 02:14:36,320
It's been days since I've eaten.

1631
02:14:36,403 --> 02:14:37,404
I told you to fuck off!

1632
02:14:37,488 --> 02:14:41,117
You can't be generous
with food that's not even yours?

1633
02:14:42,785 --> 02:14:44,161
There's no room in your bowl.

1634
02:14:44,245 --> 02:14:45,496
Yes, there is.

1635
02:14:50,709 --> 02:14:52,419
So there is.

1636
02:14:54,004 --> 02:14:55,923
There you go.

1637
02:14:59,343 --> 02:15:00,928
Suppose you want something from me now.

1638
02:15:01,011 --> 02:15:02,054
No.

1639
02:15:02,680 --> 02:15:04,431
I have something for you.

1640
02:15:05,391 --> 02:15:07,768
You have something for me?

1641
02:15:07,852 --> 02:15:09,395
He has something for me!

1642
02:15:11,272 --> 02:15:14,733
What could a filthy
old plate-licker like you

1643
02:15:14,817 --> 02:15:17,653
possibly have that I could want?

1644
02:15:17,736 --> 02:15:20,406
Actually, it's yours.

1645
02:15:25,786 --> 02:15:27,538
I was asked to return it.

1646
02:15:29,665 --> 02:15:30,708
It's not mine.

1647
02:15:32,585 --> 02:15:33,627
No?

1648
02:15:36,964 --> 02:15:38,174
Where did you get this?

1649
02:15:40,551 --> 02:15:42,386
Don't turn your back on me!

1650
02:15:47,892 --> 02:15:50,936
Antinous,
you can't treat a beggar like that.

1651
02:15:51,020 --> 02:15:52,062
It's Zeus' Law.

1652
02:15:52,646 --> 02:15:53,939
Zeus' Law.

1653
02:15:58,444 --> 02:15:59,778
You think this is a god?

1654
02:16:03,365 --> 02:16:05,242
Think this is a god in disguise?

1655
02:16:06,076 --> 02:16:07,077
Are you?

1656
02:16:08,871 --> 02:16:10,122
Are you a god,

1657
02:16:11,248 --> 02:16:12,291
plate-licker?

1658
02:16:13,459 --> 02:16:14,460
No.

1659
02:16:15,211 --> 02:16:17,296
I'm a veteran of the Trojan War.

1660
02:16:17,379 --> 02:16:20,007
Veteran of the Trojan War.

1661
02:16:22,593 --> 02:16:24,094
And what's your name, veteran?

1662
02:16:28,349 --> 02:16:29,350
Sinon.

1663
02:16:33,020 --> 02:16:34,688
Does that name mean something to you?

1664
02:16:35,648 --> 02:16:36,690
No.

1665
02:16:37,733 --> 02:16:41,862
I use it to honor the bravest
young man I ever met.

1666
02:16:42,488 --> 02:16:43,572
Sinon.

1667
02:16:45,199 --> 02:16:46,867
Wasn't that your shepherd's boy?

1668
02:16:49,411 --> 02:16:50,496
I don't remember that.

1669
02:16:50,579 --> 02:16:53,207
Yeah, yeah, he wanted you
to take his place.

1670
02:16:53,707 --> 02:16:55,042
Where did you find this?

1671
02:16:55,542 --> 02:16:56,877
Where you'll soon be.

1672
02:16:57,878 --> 02:16:58,963
And where is that?

1673
02:16:59,588 --> 02:17:00,881
Hades.

1674
02:17:04,385 --> 02:17:05,719
Enough!

1675
02:17:07,179 --> 02:17:08,847
The feast is done!

1676
02:17:13,185 --> 02:17:14,103
Let's go.

1677
02:17:15,354 --> 02:17:16,772
It's not mine!

1678
02:17:25,614 --> 02:17:27,574
The Queen would like to speak with you.

1679
02:17:27,658 --> 02:17:30,119
Stay till everyone's left
and the fires are lit.

1680
02:18:21,045 --> 02:18:22,338
Hello, traveler.

1681
02:18:23,464 --> 02:18:24,757
My Queen.

1682
02:18:24,840 --> 02:18:28,719
Melantho, have Eurycleia
wash the stranger's feet.

1683
02:18:28,802 --> 02:18:30,137
That's not necessary.

1684
02:18:30,220 --> 02:18:33,807
My husband insisted
we honor guests as family.

1685
02:18:34,433 --> 02:18:36,810
You know of Odysseus, my husband?

1686
02:18:36,894 --> 02:18:37,936
Of course.

1687
02:18:38,437 --> 02:18:39,688
Through the songs.

1688
02:18:40,272 --> 02:18:41,857
I was at Troy, ma'am.

1689
02:18:42,524 --> 02:18:44,526
Then the songs
probably seem silly.

1690
02:18:46,028 --> 02:18:47,446
They make me cry.

1691
02:18:48,489 --> 02:18:49,656
Why?

1692
02:18:49,740 --> 02:18:51,575
For what was lost.

1693
02:18:52,451 --> 02:18:53,619
The lives?

1694
02:18:54,286 --> 02:18:55,579
And the years.

1695
02:18:56,747 --> 02:18:57,748
Everything.

1696
02:19:05,381 --> 02:19:07,549
Did you know Odysseus at Troy?

1697
02:19:07,633 --> 02:19:08,801
I did.

1698
02:19:10,052 --> 02:19:13,972
Was there some detail
of how he dressed, something he wore?

1699
02:19:14,681 --> 02:19:16,642
He wore a strange pin.

1700
02:19:16,725 --> 02:19:18,268
Strange?

1701
02:19:18,811 --> 02:19:19,812
Beautiful.

1702
02:19:20,687 --> 02:19:21,897
But out of place.

1703
02:19:22,398 --> 02:19:23,399
Describe it.

1704
02:19:37,913 --> 02:19:39,498
I'll get more water.

1705
02:19:43,794 --> 02:19:46,505
It was a beautiful, gold pin.

1706
02:19:47,798 --> 02:19:50,634
A figure of Athena encircled by vines.

1707
02:19:59,351 --> 02:20:00,936
Did he wear it often?

1708
02:20:02,312 --> 02:20:03,564
He never took it off.

1709
02:20:23,959 --> 02:20:26,753
How is it that you made it home
when Odysseus could not?

1710
02:20:27,921 --> 02:20:30,632
I'm not home. Not yet.

1711
02:20:31,508 --> 02:20:32,759
Why not?

1712
02:20:32,843 --> 02:20:35,471
It's not always an easy thing,
a homecoming.

1713
02:20:36,305 --> 02:20:38,891
That's as true for Odysseus as for me.

1714
02:20:40,184 --> 02:20:41,810
The Odysseus I knew,

1715
02:20:42,895 --> 02:20:47,649
would have found or fought his way
back to me no matter what.

1716
02:20:48,567 --> 02:20:51,945
What if the Odysseus you knew
lost his way?

1717
02:20:56,492 --> 02:20:59,870
What if, one night,

1718
02:21:00,996 --> 02:21:02,414
in a strange city,

1719
02:21:04,249 --> 02:21:05,501
he saw things

1720
02:21:07,002 --> 02:21:09,463
that made him think the home he knew

1721
02:21:10,797 --> 02:21:12,841
couldn't possibly be there anymore?

1722
02:21:16,887 --> 02:21:17,763
What if,

1723
02:21:18,388 --> 02:21:20,307
when he left the belly of the horse

1724
02:21:24,645 --> 02:21:26,480
and opened the gates of Troy,

1725
02:21:31,944 --> 02:21:34,696
he saw ten years of rage

1726
02:21:34,780 --> 02:21:37,616
pour into that city in one night?

1727
02:22:25,080 --> 02:22:27,499
Everyone, now!

1728
02:23:53,377 --> 02:23:56,463
We left them a gift.

1729
02:23:56,546 --> 02:23:59,216
An offering of peace.

1730
02:23:59,299 --> 02:24:02,552
That they took into their home.

1731
02:24:12,771 --> 02:24:15,607
We violated all that's ever sacred

1732
02:24:16,942 --> 02:24:18,527
between people.

1733
02:24:20,487 --> 02:24:21,530
And turned a fight...

1734
02:24:22,489 --> 02:24:23,699
Destroy their idols!

1735
02:24:25,492 --> 02:24:26,743
...into a hunt.

1736
02:24:31,081 --> 02:24:32,082
Please don't.

1737
02:24:42,718 --> 02:24:44,636
To burn the walls of Troy

1738
02:24:45,262 --> 02:24:47,723
was to burn the world entire.

1739
02:24:49,474 --> 02:24:51,143
Including his home.

1740
02:24:57,899 --> 02:24:59,067
What if he knew

1741
02:25:00,902 --> 02:25:02,237
that very night...

1742
02:25:05,407 --> 02:25:09,703
...as he walked through fires
of anarchy and pain...

1743
02:25:58,960 --> 02:26:02,380
...and in the days of sweaty
celebrations to follow...

1744
02:26:08,720 --> 02:26:11,932
...what if he knew exactly what he'd done?

1745
02:26:30,575 --> 02:26:32,369
What all of you had done.

1746
02:26:32,452 --> 02:26:34,120
One man's idea.

1747
02:26:35,622 --> 02:26:37,123
One man's trick.

1748
02:26:38,750 --> 02:26:41,461
To break Zeus' Law forever.

1749
02:26:42,838 --> 02:26:46,007
We lived in a world of palaces and trade.

1750
02:26:47,676 --> 02:26:48,844
And language.

1751
02:26:48,927 --> 02:26:50,720
Blind to its beauty.

1752
02:26:51,680 --> 02:26:53,306
Until we broke it.

1753
02:26:55,725 --> 02:26:57,936
You are the people from the sea?

1754
02:27:00,522 --> 02:27:01,648
Yes, my Queen.

1755
02:27:03,400 --> 02:27:06,736
The breaking of Zeus' Law
is spreading like plague.

1756
02:27:06,820 --> 02:27:08,488
Our age of bronze is collapsing.

1757
02:27:08,572 --> 02:27:12,200
And maybe he couldn't bear to see
the ruins of what he'd done.

1758
02:27:13,034 --> 02:27:14,035
Anywhere.

1759
02:27:14,578 --> 02:27:16,621
Least of all, his home.

1760
02:27:26,214 --> 02:27:27,966
But Odysseus is alive.

1761
02:27:29,759 --> 02:27:31,928
And he'll be here when you need him most.

1762
02:27:34,055 --> 02:27:35,223
Well,

1763
02:27:36,558 --> 02:27:39,394
I've been living in his ruins long enough.

1764
02:27:44,649 --> 02:27:46,151
Bring the suitors at dawn.

1765
02:27:46,234 --> 02:27:48,278
Remove the arms from the hall.

1766
02:27:48,361 --> 02:27:52,616
In a few hours, one suitor will be happy
and the rest angry, vengeful men.

1767
02:27:52,699 --> 02:27:54,159
Bronze draws a man on,

1768
02:27:54,242 --> 02:27:57,120
so we'll have no weapons at the trial
and the doors will be locked.

1769
02:27:57,996 --> 02:28:00,248
I'll marry whoever completes my challenge.

1770
02:28:06,963 --> 02:28:08,590
How will we take on the suitors?

1771
02:28:09,549 --> 02:28:10,800
Not "we."

1772
02:28:11,343 --> 02:28:13,303
Their blood belongs on my hands.

1773
02:28:13,887 --> 02:28:15,305
Or you face exile.

1774
02:28:16,348 --> 02:28:17,474
Won't you?

1775
02:28:18,391 --> 02:28:20,727
Just make sure they can't get
those weapons.

1776
02:28:20,810 --> 02:28:22,020
I'll see to it.

1777
02:28:22,771 --> 02:28:24,773
Even unarmed, there are so many of them.

1778
02:28:25,565 --> 02:28:27,108
And you won't have a weapon.

1779
02:28:28,568 --> 02:28:30,028
Oh, I'll have a weapon.

1780
02:28:31,738 --> 02:28:33,114
Your mother's seen to that.

1781
02:28:35,700 --> 02:28:37,953
Gather the servants you trust.

1782
02:28:38,036 --> 02:28:39,871
Lock the arms in the upper storage room.

1783
02:28:39,955 --> 02:28:41,081
Yes, I'll see to it.

1784
02:28:59,265 --> 02:29:01,601
Hurry, hurry.
Get the weapons upstairs.

1785
02:29:09,234 --> 02:29:10,443
Hurry, hurry.

1786
02:29:33,133 --> 02:29:35,093
Zeus seems interested in the outcome.

1787
02:29:35,176 --> 02:29:36,553
But what's the trial?

1788
02:30:01,036 --> 02:30:02,454
For years,

1789
02:30:03,580 --> 02:30:05,206
while my husband's been gone,

1790
02:30:05,957 --> 02:30:08,793
you've commandeered
this palace for your feasting.

1791
02:30:09,461 --> 02:30:12,672
Your excuse was you wanted
to win my hand in marriage.

1792
02:30:16,301 --> 02:30:20,221
As I listened to you party and fight,

1793
02:30:22,557 --> 02:30:26,895
corrupt my servants
and disrespect my home,

1794
02:30:26,978 --> 02:30:29,272
I've wanted nothing more
than for Odysseus to return

1795
02:30:29,355 --> 02:30:31,066
and see all this for himself.

1796
02:30:38,031 --> 02:30:40,992
But, last night, I realized

1797
02:30:41,076 --> 02:30:42,368
he's not here now.

1798
02:30:46,122 --> 02:30:47,332
He never will be.

1799
02:30:49,292 --> 02:30:50,418
So,

1800
02:30:51,211 --> 02:30:54,422
I stand ready to marry anyone here

1801
02:30:54,506 --> 02:30:59,803
who can string this hunting bow
and shoot an arrow through those 12 axes.

1802
02:31:01,596 --> 02:31:03,098
Step up.

1803
02:31:04,933 --> 02:31:06,226
And show me who you are.

1804
02:31:07,852 --> 02:31:08,853
Eumaeus.

1805
02:31:20,615 --> 02:31:22,117
Who's first?

1806
02:31:22,200 --> 02:31:23,284
Give me the bow.

1807
02:31:43,972 --> 02:31:45,014
It can't be done.

1808
02:31:45,098 --> 02:31:46,474
By you.

1809
02:31:46,558 --> 02:31:48,726
As a boy, I saw Odysseus do it with ease.

1810
02:31:48,810 --> 02:31:49,853
Then you do it.

1811
02:31:50,812 --> 02:31:51,813
I'll wait my turn.

1812
02:31:53,106 --> 02:31:55,358
Why don't we warm the bow and oil it?

1813
02:31:56,818 --> 02:31:58,778
This bow hasn't been strung in years.

1814
02:32:32,270 --> 02:32:33,313
Your turn.

1815
02:32:33,396 --> 02:32:34,731
There's no need.

1816
02:32:38,026 --> 02:32:39,527
My Queen has made her point.

1817
02:32:39,611 --> 02:32:41,571
We can't replace Odysseus.

1818
02:32:41,654 --> 02:32:43,281
Some of us have always known that.

1819
02:32:43,364 --> 02:32:46,284
Some of us would have followed
that great man to Troy.

1820
02:32:46,367 --> 02:32:47,410
If he'd let us.

1821
02:32:50,246 --> 02:32:51,748
But he never came back.

1822
02:32:53,666 --> 02:32:55,251
So choose the best of us here.

1823
02:32:56,920 --> 02:32:59,255
All of us who've failed your test.

1824
02:32:59,839 --> 02:33:01,007
I haven't.

1825
02:33:05,887 --> 02:33:07,555
Somebody get these beggars out of here!

1826
02:33:08,723 --> 02:33:11,643
The doors are locked
until the trial's over.

1827
02:33:11,726 --> 02:33:14,270
- The trial is over.
- Yeah.

1828
02:33:14,354 --> 02:33:15,396
I wanna try.

1829
02:33:15,480 --> 02:33:17,607
Give it a rest, old man.

1830
02:33:18,775 --> 02:33:20,610
No, he has a right to try.

1831
02:33:21,819 --> 02:33:23,947
If you won't, he may as well.

1832
02:33:24,447 --> 02:33:26,407
You're making a mockery
of your mother's wishes.

1833
02:33:26,491 --> 02:33:28,910
My mother's wishes
are for you all to burn.

1834
02:33:40,505 --> 02:33:42,757
He's an old archer.

1835
02:33:46,010 --> 02:33:47,220
Come on.

1836
02:33:47,845 --> 02:33:49,931
Look at the expert at work.

1837
02:33:50,014 --> 02:33:52,058
Plate-licker!

1838
02:33:52,850 --> 02:33:53,977
Come on!

1839
02:33:54,060 --> 02:33:55,728
Old man, forget it!

1840
02:33:56,771 --> 02:33:57,897
Hey, old man!

1841
02:33:58,606 --> 02:34:00,024
Come on!

1842
02:34:07,156 --> 02:34:08,324
Okay, friend.

1843
02:34:08,825 --> 02:34:09,951
Friend, that's enough.

1844
02:34:10,034 --> 02:34:11,286
Let him try.

1845
02:34:11,369 --> 02:34:13,037
- Let him try.
- Come on.

1846
02:34:54,912 --> 02:34:56,247
You're Odyss...

1847
02:35:02,128 --> 02:35:04,839
Surely your place is down there.

1848
02:35:18,853 --> 02:35:19,854
Is it him?

1849
02:35:20,897 --> 02:35:21,731
It's him.

1850
02:35:31,699 --> 02:35:32,825
Go secure the weapons.

1851
02:35:47,465 --> 02:35:48,883
Where are the weapons?

1852
02:35:48,966 --> 02:35:49,967
The upper store room.

1853
02:35:50,051 --> 02:35:51,052
Get them!

1854
02:35:55,264 --> 02:35:58,518
What are you waiting for?
He's gonna kill us all!

1855
02:36:01,979 --> 02:36:03,231
Look out!

1856
02:36:08,444 --> 02:36:10,905
I told you. Too loud, cowherd.

1857
02:36:10,988 --> 02:36:12,949
No! No! No!

1858
02:36:13,574 --> 02:36:16,285
Eumaeus! Let me out!

1859
02:36:21,624 --> 02:36:22,625
Down!

1860
02:36:33,469 --> 02:36:34,679
No!

1861
02:37:29,859 --> 02:37:31,194
Get the swords!

1862
02:38:31,045 --> 02:38:33,130
Get the shields! Get the shields!

1863
02:39:12,128 --> 02:39:13,421
Spears!

1864
02:39:43,326 --> 02:39:44,702
Use the spears!

1865
02:40:38,756 --> 02:40:40,132
Thought you could kill us all?

1866
02:40:40,633 --> 02:40:42,259
Long as I kill you.

1867
02:40:43,844 --> 02:40:44,887
Finish this.

1868
02:40:48,057 --> 02:40:49,475
Dad!

1869
02:41:19,338 --> 02:41:20,339
When you get to Hades,

1870
02:41:22,842 --> 02:41:26,470
tell my men I will head west
and honor them.

1871
02:41:32,518 --> 02:41:33,519
And tell Sinon

1872
02:41:36,313 --> 02:41:38,733
I gave you back your shame.

1873
02:41:58,085 --> 02:41:59,462
What happened?

1874
02:42:00,629 --> 02:42:01,839
Did he beat them?

1875
02:42:03,090 --> 02:42:04,425
Is he dead?

1876
02:42:06,218 --> 02:42:08,888
We broke
the fragile bonds between men.

1877
02:42:17,563 --> 02:42:20,649
And, for centuries
of darkness to follow,

1878
02:42:20,733 --> 02:42:24,612
the stories of Troy will only be sung...

1879
02:43:44,567 --> 02:43:45,568
Odysseus, my love.

1880
02:43:46,902 --> 02:43:47,903
Please, my love.

1881
02:43:49,780 --> 02:43:51,532
Odysseus.

1882
02:43:51,615 --> 02:43:54,034
My love. You came back.

1883
02:43:55,703 --> 02:43:56,912
At last.

1884
02:43:57,621 --> 02:43:58,706
At the end.

1885
02:44:00,249 --> 02:44:04,295
To see my beloved Ithaca. One last time.

1886
02:44:05,254 --> 02:44:06,755
No. No.

1887
02:44:06,839 --> 02:44:07,840
No, no, you can't. You can't.

1888
02:44:07,923 --> 02:44:09,633
You can't. Not now. Not now.

1889
02:44:09,717 --> 02:44:11,135
Please. I've waited so long.

1890
02:44:13,596 --> 02:44:14,930
You can't die.

1891
02:44:16,223 --> 02:44:17,308
Not death.

1892
02:44:19,351 --> 02:44:20,644
Exile.

1893
02:44:28,903 --> 02:44:30,654
Telemachus will be King.

1894
02:44:34,325 --> 02:44:36,201
We'll head into the unknown west.

1895
02:44:37,202 --> 02:44:38,746
To honor my men.

1896
02:44:40,331 --> 02:44:41,457
Together.

1897
02:44:42,082 --> 02:44:43,918
Take your fastest ship...

1898
02:44:44,668 --> 02:44:46,712
...and brightest crew.

1899
02:44:46,795 --> 02:44:48,756
And head for the horizon.

1900
02:44:49,757 --> 02:44:51,800
Chasing the escaping sun.

1901
02:44:51,884 --> 02:44:54,261
A face...

1902
02:44:54,345 --> 02:44:56,263
Why will the stories only be sung?

1903
02:44:59,308 --> 02:45:02,728
Because songs will be all they have
to remember those of us who could write.

1904
02:45:03,896 --> 02:45:06,273
...a war...

1905
02:45:07,650 --> 02:45:09,902
Civilization will rise again.

1906
02:45:09,985 --> 02:45:12,279
...a man...

1907
02:45:15,908 --> 02:45:18,744
A new dawn will break
over the darkened world.

1908
02:45:22,164 --> 02:45:24,208
And our mistakes
will once again be forgotten.

